# check=skip=FromPlatformFlagConstDisallowed
#
# ^ A BuildKit parser directive, and it MUST be the first line of the file --
#   directives are only recognised before any comment or instruction.
#
#   It silences exactly one advisory lint, `FromPlatformFlagConstDisallowed`,
#   which suggests `FROM --platform=$BUILDPLATFORM` instead of a literal. That
#   advice is written for images intended to build for many architectures; this
#   one is deliberately single-architecture. The compiled COBOL this image
#   produces IS the behavioural specification, and COBOL numeric storage is
#   representation-sensitive -- `COMP`/`BINARY-LONG` fields have a byte order and
#   a width, `COMP-3` is packed decimal -- while the Python side installed here
#   is an x86_64 wheel closure. `$BUILDPLATFORM` would make the arbiter's
#   architecture a property of whoever ran the build, which is the exact drift
#   the pin exists to prevent. The lint is therefore skipped by name, in one
#   place, with this reason recorded, rather than left to be re-litigated as
#   noise on every build. Nothing else is skipped: every other check must stay
#   clean.
#

# =============================================================================
#  harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol
#  The GnuCOBOL half of the compiled-COBOL ORACLE for the ACAS posting-cycle
#  migration (COBOL -> Python 3.12).
# =============================================================================
#
#  PURPOSE
#  -------
#  This image is the ARBITER. The migration has no pre-existing test suite:
#  compiled COBOL execution IS the behavioural specification, defects included,
#  and a defect reproduced is correct while a defect fixed is a failure. This
#  image is therefore the one place in the project that contains `cobc`, and it
#  exists so that a semantic question about the posting cycle can be settled by
#  RUNNING the maintainer's own programs rather than by reasoning about them.
#
#  It carries two things:
#    1. the maintainer's exact COBOL toolchain -- GnuCOBOL 3.2 final, the MySQL
#       client libraries under /usr/local/mysql, the JC preSQL translator
#       `presql2`, and the bridge's C interface object `cobmysqlapi.o`; and
#    2. CPython 3.12 plus the repository's exactly pinned Python dependencies,
#       so the Python side of every comparison runs on the same host, against
#       the same database, in the same process family.
#
#  Because both cycles are driven from this single image against the single
#  MariaDB served by the sibling `harness/Dockerfile.mariadb`, a non-empty
#  ordering-normalised table diff is always a REAL behavioural difference and
#  never an artefact of the comparison. That property is the whole point.
#
#  POSITION IN THE TREE
#  --------------------
#  `harness/` is the compiled-COBOL oracle tree and is a SIBLING of
#  `acas_posting/`, never a sub-package -- the plan annotates it as "the
#  compiled oracle; NEVER on the package import path". There is no
#  `harness/__init__.py`, this image creates none, and nothing under `harness/`
#  is importable: `pyproject.toml` packages `acas_posting*` only and excludes
#  `harness*`. The harness Python tools (`dump_tables.py`, `normalize.py`,
#  `diff_states.py`) are standalone scripts invoked BY PATH.
#
#  WHY THIS IMAGE ALSO CARRIES CPYTHON -- AND WHY THAT IS NOT AN R-1 VIOLATION
#  --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  R-1 ("no COBOL at runtime") constrains the SHIPPED Python package's import
#  path and the shipped artifact -- not a harness container. The plan specifies
#  a TWO-service compose file ("MariaDB service at the version the schema was
#  produced by, plus a GnuCOBOL builder service"), so `run_python_scenario.sh`,
#  `dump_tables.py`, `normalize.py` and `diff_states.py` necessarily execute
#  here as well. The prohibition R-1 protects is preserved structurally and is
#  verifiable rather than promised:
#    * `pyproject.toml` excludes `harness*` from packaging, so the built wheel
#      carries no COBOL, no `cobc` requirement and no `cobmysqlapi.o` linkage;
#    * nothing in this file creates a `harness` package or puts `harness/` on
#      `PYTHONPATH`;
#    * `acas_posting/` contains no subprocess call, no FFI bridge and no
#      linkage to the bridge's C interface object -- the compiled oracle is
#      reached only OUT OF PROCESS, from the scenario and determinism suites.
#  The complementary half of the same separation is enforced in the sibling
#  image: grep `harness/Dockerfile.mariadb` and you will find no COBOL
#  toolchain component of any kind.
#
#  BUILD CONTEXT -- READ THIS BEFORE BUILDING
#  ------------------------------------------
#  The build context MUST be the REPOSITORY ROOT, not `harness/`, because this
#  file copies three artifacts that only resolve relative to the repository
#  root: `requirements.txt`, `presql2-latest.zip` and
#  `mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip`. `harness/docker-compose.yml` declares
#  `context: ..` with `dockerfile: harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol` precisely so
#  those copies resolve, and names the service `gnucobol`.
#
#      Preferred:  docker compose -f harness/docker-compose.yml build gnucobol
#      Equivalent: docker build -f harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol \
#                      -t acas-oracle-gnucobol .      # <-- run from repo root
#
#  Building with `harness/` as the context FAILS at the first COPY, by design.
#  That failure is a feature: the contract enforces itself.
#
#  THE COMPILE RECIPE IS FROZEN -- R-4, AND THE MOST IMPORTANT RULE HERE
#  --------------------------------------------------------------------
#  The compiler must be invoked with DEFAULT ARITHMETIC, and this image must
#  not alter that. A census of every `cobc` invocation reachable by the oracle
#  build finds NO `-std=` dialect selection, NO `>>SET ARITHMETIC` directive in
#  any source, and NO `binary-truncate` flag anywhere. The flags the frozen
#  scripts actually use are exactly:
#      -m  -x  -Wlinkage  -I ../copybooks  cobmysqlapi.o
#      -L/usr/local/mysql/lib   [common/comp-common.sh:L26]
#      -L /usr/local/mysql/lib  (space variant) [common/comp-common.sh:L32,L36]
#      -lmysqlclient  -lz  -T <name>.prn        [common/comp-common.sh:L26,L51]
#      -D_FORTIFY_SOURCE=1  -fdump=ws  -fmissing-statement=ok
#                                               [general/comp-gl.sh:L2-L5]
#      -lncursesw -A '-DHAVE_NCURSESW_NCURSES_H -DDECIMAL_RIGHT'
#                                               [common/comp-common.sh:L18]
#  Nothing in this image adds to, removes from or re-spells that list. In
#  particular it does not enable `-g -d`, `-fdump=ALL` or `-ftraceall`, which
#  appear only in the `*-diags*.sh` script variants the harness never runs.
#
#  TWO STALE-RECORD DEFECTS THAT ARE DELIBERATELY *NOT* FIXED
#  ----------------------------------------------------------
#  A defect reproduced is correct; a defect fixed is a failure. Both of the
#  following are inconsistencies between the maintainer's changelog comments
#  and his live code. Both are left exactly as they are:
#
#    1. [common/comp-common.sh:L8-L9] records that `-Wno-goto-section` was
#       "added to remove the silly default warning in latest gnucobol v3.2",
#       and [README.TXT:L204-L212] says the same. It was NOT added. A repo-wide
#       search finds `-Wno-goto-section` in exactly three places, all of them
#       changelog comments -- [common/comp-common.sh:L8],
#       [common/comp-common2.sh:L8] and
#       [common/comp-common-no-rdbms-diags.sh:L9] -- and in ZERO live `cobc`
#       invocations. This image does not add it. Adding a flag alters the
#       recipe, and the inter-section `GO TO` warnings it would suppress are
#       diagnostics only: they change no computed value and no table state.
#
#    2. [common/comp-common.sh:L11] claims "Added to all comps -fdump=all", yet
#       no live line in that file uses `-fdump` at all. (The General Ledger
#       script does use `-fdump=ws` -- a different thing -- at
#       [general/comp-gl.sh:L2-L5].) Left as found.
#
#  Two further asymmetries are noted here so that no one "harmonises" them:
#  [common/comp-common.sh:L59] and [comp-all.sh:L44-L45] both end with an
#  unconditional `exit 0`, so NEITHER script's exit status is a build-success
#  signal -- `build_oracle.sh` must scan captured output instead, which is why
#  this image neither wraps, colourises, filters nor reorders their output; and
#  `sales/comp-sales.sh` alone omits `-fdump=ws`/`-fmissing-statement=ok` and
#  has its `accept_numeric` lines commented out. All left as found.
#
#  THE CHECKOUT IS MOUNTED READ-ONLY -- NOTHING HERE WRITES TO IT
#  --------------------------------------------------------------
#  The frozen artifacts are read in place from `$ACAS_REPO` (/repo), mounted
#  READ-ONLY, and `build_oracle.sh` copies them to the writable `$ACAS_BUILD`
#  (/build) before compiling. This is not fastidiousness; running the frozen
#  scripts in the checkout would damage it five separate ways:
#    1. [common/comp-common.sh:L25] runs `presql2` over every `*MT.scb`, which
#       OVERWRITES the committed, frozen generated bridges `common/*MT.cbl`;
#    2. every compile passes `-T <name>.prn`, creating listing files;
#    3. `cobmysqlapi.o` must sit in the CWD of every compile, so six copies
#       would land in six frozen directories;
#    4. `presql2.param` would appear as a new file in the CWD;
#    5. `cobc -m` / `-x` outputs land in the source directories.
#  Any diff touching the frozen tree is a defect in the migration regardless of
#  how harmless it looks. Accordingly: this image contains no COPY and no RUN
#  that writes anywhere under /repo, and its WORKDIR is /build, never /repo.
#  It also embeds no copy of any `.cbl`, `.scb`, `.cob` or `.sql` file.
#
#  DIVISION OF LABOUR WITH build_oracle.sh -- FIXED, AND STATED IDENTICALLY
#  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#  The oracle bootstrap has five steps. Steps 1-3 are performed HERE, at image
#  build time, and their artifacts are published at documented paths:
#      1. unpack the vendored `presql2-latest.zip`  -> /opt/presql2-package
#      2. compile the bridge's C interface object   -> /usr/local/lib/acas/cobmysqlapi.o
#      3. build and install the translator          -> /usr/local/bin/presql2
#  Steps 4-5 belong to `harness/build_oracle.sh`, at run time, because they
#  need a live database and a writable copy of the checkout:
#      4. run `common/comp-common.sh` to translate and compile the bridges,
#         handlers and loaders
#      5. run `comp-all.sh`, which compiles the application directories in the
#         maintainer's own order: common, general, irs, purchase, sales, stock
#         [comp-all.sh:L15-L32]  (OE/payroll/epos are commented out there)
#  `build_oracle.sh` re-runs steps 1-3 only if these artifacts are absent, and
#  copies `cobmysqlapi.o` into each of those six directories because every
#  frozen compile line links a BARE `cobmysqlapi.o` from the current directory.
#
#  DETERMINISM
#  -----------
#  `tests/determinism/test_two_runs_byte_identical.py` requires two runs of the
#  same scenario under the same pinned clock to produce byte-identical dumps.
#  Nothing here bakes in a wall-clock timestamp, a build date, a hostname, a
#  VCS revision or a run identifier, and every version is pinned -- including
#  the GnuCOBOL tarball, by SHA-256 -- so the oracle's verdict cannot drift
#  between builds.
#
#  NO CREDENTIALS
#  --------------
#  No credential literal appears anywhere in this file, including in comments.
#  `ACAS_DB_USER` and `ACAS_DB_PASSWORD` are deliberately NOT declared as ENV at
#  all -- not even empty. BuildKit's `SecretsUsedInArgOrEnv` lint fires on a
#  credential-shaped ENV name regardless of its value, and it is right to: an
#  ENV entry is permanent image metadata that `docker inspect` exposes. The two
#  names are documented instead, here and at the ENV block, so the contract
#  stays greppable without entering a layer. `harness/docker-compose.yml`
#  supplies both at run time, exactly as the sibling `harness/Dockerfile.mariadb`
#  does for its own `MARIADB_*` set.
#  The vendored `presql2-package/presql2.param` DOES carry example credential
#  literals; this image neither copies it to a working directory nor reproduces
#  its values -- only its six-card SHAPE is documented, in placeholder form.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  BASE IMAGE -- PINNED BY CONTENT DIGEST AND PINNED TO ONE ARCHITECTURE
#
#  Pinned to the Ubuntu LTS release the plan records as the reference
#  environment: Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, whose system CPython is 3.12.3 with a
#  `decimal` module backed by the C libmpdec implementation rather than the
#  pure-Python fallback. That is not a convenience -- every monetary and
#  quantity operation in the migrated cycle passes through `decimal.Decimal`.
#
#  THE TAG ALONE IS NOT A PIN. `ubuntu:24.04` is a MOVING tag: Canonical
#  republishes it on every point release and every security rebuild, so two
#  builds of the same repository revision, a fortnight apart, would produce two
#  different oracles. The digest below is the OCI image INDEX digest of that tag
#  as resolved and verified on this host; the tag is retained beside it purely as
#  human-readable documentation, while the digest decides what is actually
#  pulled. An arbiter that changes underneath the comparison is not an arbiter,
#  and [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L1] is unambiguous that the reference platform is
#  x86_64 -- so the architecture is fixed here too, for three concrete reasons:
#
#    1. COBOL numeric storage is representation-sensitive. `COMP` and
#       `BINARY-LONG` fields have a byte order and a width -- the seven
#       `binary-long` statistics fields at [copybooks/wssl.cob:L46-L52] are
#       exactly the ones whose INTEGER truncation the migration must reproduce --
#       and `COMP-3` is packed decimal. The bridge's recovered C interface
#       [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.c] moves those bytes between COBOL host
#       variables and the client library.
#    2. The Python dependency closure in `requirements.txt` was resolved as
#       x86_64 wheels, including the C extension of `mysql-connector-python`.
#    3. An oracle built for a different architecture would be comparing two
#       different machines, which would make a non-empty diff an artefact of the
#       comparison -- precisely what AAP 0.6.6 forbids.
#
#  Neither pin is a build argument, and that is deliberate: an overrideable pin
#  is not a pin. See the BUILD-TIME PINS section immediately below, where the
#  same argument is applied to every toolchain constant.
#
#  RESOLVING THE DIGEST. The value below is the MULTI-ARCH INDEX digest, read
#  with
#
#      docker buildx imagetools inspect ubuntu:24.04
#
#  and NOT the per-PLATFORM digest that `docker manifest inspect --verbose`
#  reports. Both values look equally plausible, so the trap is named here; the
#  architecture is fixed by the `--platform` flag rather than by narrowing the
#  digest.
#
#  The interpreter assertion in the Python layer below is the backstop: because
#  CPython arrives from the base image, a re-pin that moved the base off
#  CPython 3.12.x would move the interpreter under the exact-decimal core, and
#  the build fails at that assertion rather than producing a subtly wrong
#  arbiter.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
FROM --platform=linux/amd64 ubuntu:24.04@sha256:4fbb8e6a8395de5a7550b33509421a2bafbc0aab6c06ba2cef9ebffbc7092d90

LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="ACAS posting-cycle oracle - GnuCOBOL" \
      org.opencontainers.image.description="GnuCOBOL 3.2 final, MySQL client libraries under /usr/local/mysql, the JC preSQL translator and CPython 3.12, for compiling and driving the compiled-COBOL oracle of the ACAS posting-cycle migration." \
      org.opencontainers.image.licenses="GPL-3.0-or-later" \
      org.opencontainers.image.base.name="docker.io/library/ubuntu:24.04@sha256:4fbb8e6a8395de5a7550b33509421a2bafbc0aab6c06ba2cef9ebffbc7092d90" \
      org.opencontainers.image.base.digest="sha256:4fbb8e6a8395de5a7550b33509421a2bafbc0aab6c06ba2cef9ebffbc7092d90" \
      acas.harness.role="oracle-builder-and-runner" \
      acas.harness.cobc="3.2" \
      acas.harness.mysql.prefix="/usr/local/mysql" \
      acas.harness.python="3.12" \
      acas.harness.platform="linux/amd64" \
      acas.harness.apt.snapshot="20260801T000000Z" \
      acas.harness.runtime.user="acas"


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  BUILD-TIME PINS -- DECLARATIVE, EXACT, AND IMMUTABLE
#
#  Declared as ARGs so a reviewer can see every external input in one place and
#  a maintainer can audit or mirror them. All are exact; none is a range, a
#  branch or a "latest".
#
#  BUT AN ARG ALONE IS NOT A PIN -- IT IS A DEFAULT. Every value below defines
#  the ORACLE CONTRACT: which compiler arbitrates behaviour, which client
#  library the frozen `-lmysqlclient` resolves against, which translator
#  regenerates the bridges, and where the frozen scripts expect to find them.
#  `--build-arg GNUCOBOL_VERSION=3.1.2` would silently produce a DIFFERENT
#  arbiter from the SAME repository revision -- and because the compiled COBOL
#  *is* the behavioural specification, "different arbiter" means "different
#  specification", not merely "different build".
#
#  They are therefore GATED: the step immediately after this block compares
#  every one of them against a hard-coded literal and FAILS THE BUILD on any
#  deviation. Overriding a pin is still possible, but only the way the AAP
#  intends -- by editing this file in a reviewable commit, where the change to
#  the oracle contract is visible in the diff.
#
#  `DEBIAN_FRONTEND` further below is deliberately NOT gated: it controls apt's
#  prompting behaviour during the build and is not part of the oracle contract.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

#  GnuCOBOL 3.2 FINAL. The version is repository evidence, not a preference:
#  [common/comp-common.sh:L8-L9]  "... in latest gnucobol v3.2."
#  [README.TXT:L53]  "... migrated over to using the GnuCobol compiler
#                     (3.2 final) now some years back."
#  [README.TXT:L168] "For development and testing I am now using v3.2 final
#                     instead of playing with 3.3 A/Beta etc ..."
#  Distribution packages are commonly 3.1.x or 4.x and are therefore NOT used;
#  the release tarball is fetched from the GNU mirror. The build then makes two
#  refusals of its own rather than trusting the URL: the digest below must
#  match the download, and `configure` must carry PACKAGE_STRING='GnuCOBOL
#  3.2', which a 3.2rc1/rc2 tarball does not. Both abort with a named FATAL.
ARG GNUCOBOL_VERSION=3.2
ARG GNUCOBOL_TARBALL=gnucobol-3.2.tar.xz
ARG GNUCOBOL_URL=https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/gnucobol/gnucobol-3.2.tar.xz
ARG GNUCOBOL_SHA256=3bb48af46ced4779facf41fdc2ee60e4ccb86eaa99d010b36685315df39c2ee2

#  MariaDB Connector/C, vendored in the checkout as
#  `mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip`. See the CLIENT LIBRARY layer below for
#  why this is the connector used and MySQL Connector/C 6.1.11 is not.
ARG MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_VERSION=3.3.4
ARG MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP=mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip

#  JC preSQL, vendored as `presql2-latest.zip` (61 zip entries). TWO version
#  strings coexist inside that archive and BOTH are correct facts about it:
#  archive member `README.SVN` line 22, "presql release (currently 1.14f)", and
#  member `presql2.cbl` line 302, `03 ws-Prog-Version pic x(6) value " 2.22 ".`
#  Recorded, not resolved -- reconciling them would be an invention.
#  A CREDENTIAL FILE IS A PREREQUISITE OF STEP 4, NOT OF THIS IMAGE. The
#  translator links the same C interface, whose `read_params` (member
#  `cobmysqlapi38.c`, line 114) `fopen`s `<progname>.param`, `exit(5)`s when it
#  is absent and `exit(6)`s on an unrecognised card. `presql2` also queries a
#  LIVE `information_schema`, so it cannot run at build time. Only the six-card
#  SHAPE is recorded -- DBHOST, DBUSER, DBPASSWD, DBNAME, DBPORT, DBSOCKET; the
#  archived example's literal values are never copied into this image.
ARG PRESQL2_ZIP=presql2-latest.zip
ARG PRESQL2_PACKAGE_VERSION=1.14f
ARG PRESQL2_PROGRAM_VERSION=2.22

#  Where the frozen scripts require the client library and headers to be. Not
#  a choice: [common/comp-common.sh:L26] hard-codes `-L/usr/local/mysql/lib`,
#  and the recovered build rule -- archive member `cobmysqlapi38.sh` --
#  hard-codes `-I/usr/local/mysql/include`.
ARG MYSQL_PREFIX=/usr/local/mysql

#  Where GnuCOBOL is installed. Also not a free choice: lines 1 and 2 of the
#  repository's own loader configuration are `/usr/local/lib/gnucobol` and
#  `/usr/local/lib` [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf], which are exactly where
#  a source build with `--prefix=/usr/local` places libcob and the default
#  module search directory. The frozen config is independent evidence that a
#  source build into /usr/local is what this system expects.
ARG GNUCOBOL_PREFIX=/usr/local

#  Canonical publication path for the bridge's C interface object (step 2).
ARG ACAS_LIB_DIR=/usr/local/lib/acas

#  The Ubuntu archive SNAPSHOT every package below is resolved from. See the
#  SYSTEM PACKAGES layer for the full argument; in short, `apt-get install`
#  against the live archive is a moving target, and a moving target under an
#  arbiter is a defect. This identifier is a FIXED, IMMUTABLE archive coordinate
#  -- it is emphatically NOT a build timestamp: it does not change from build to
#  build, so it cannot leak into anything the harness emits and cannot perturb
#  the byte-identical-dump guarantee.
ARG APT_SNAPSHOT=20260801T000000Z


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  PIN IMMUTABILITY GATE
#
#  This is the step that turns the defaults above into pins. It re-states every
#  oracle-contract value as a literal and compares it with the ARG actually in
#  effect, so `--build-arg <anything>=<other value>` fails here, loudly, at the
#  very start of the build rather than producing a quietly different oracle.
#
#  Why a gate rather than simply hard-coding the values into each later RUN: the
#  eight build steps that follow reference these names a few dozen times, and
#  inlining literals into each would multiply the number of places a future edit
#  must keep consistent. ARG values are fixed for the whole build, so a single
#  gate immediately after the declarations is sufficient AND sound -- if the gate
#  passes, every later reference necessarily sees the gated value.
#
#  The gate deliberately does NOT accept "equivalent" values. `/usr/local/mysql/`
#  with a trailing slash, or `3.2.0` for `3.2`, would each be plausible and each
#  would break something downstream -- the frozen scripts hard-code the former
#  [common/comp-common.sh:L26] and the tarball self-report test greps for the
#  latter exactly. Equality is the only safe comparison here.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN set -eu; \
    fail=0; \
    check() { \
        if [ "$2" != "$3" ]; then \
            echo "FATAL: build argument $1 was overridden." >&2; \
            echo "       expected (oracle contract) : $3" >&2; \
            echo "       received                   : $2" >&2; \
            fail=1; \
        fi; \
    }; \
    check GNUCOBOL_VERSION            "${GNUCOBOL_VERSION}"            '3.2'; \
    check GNUCOBOL_TARBALL            "${GNUCOBOL_TARBALL}"            'gnucobol-3.2.tar.xz'; \
    check GNUCOBOL_URL                "${GNUCOBOL_URL}"                'https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/gnucobol/gnucobol-3.2.tar.xz'; \
    check GNUCOBOL_SHA256             "${GNUCOBOL_SHA256}"             '3bb48af46ced4779facf41fdc2ee60e4ccb86eaa99d010b36685315df39c2ee2'; \
    check MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_VERSION "${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_VERSION}" '3.3.4'; \
    check MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP     "${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP}"     'mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip'; \
    check PRESQL2_ZIP                 "${PRESQL2_ZIP}"                 'presql2-latest.zip'; \
    check PRESQL2_PACKAGE_VERSION     "${PRESQL2_PACKAGE_VERSION}"     '1.14f'; \
    check PRESQL2_PROGRAM_VERSION     "${PRESQL2_PROGRAM_VERSION}"     '2.22'; \
    check MYSQL_PREFIX                "${MYSQL_PREFIX}"                '/usr/local/mysql'; \
    check GNUCOBOL_PREFIX             "${GNUCOBOL_PREFIX}"             '/usr/local'; \
    check ACAS_LIB_DIR                "${ACAS_LIB_DIR}"                '/usr/local/lib/acas'; \
    check APT_SNAPSHOT                "${APT_SNAPSHOT}"                '20260801T000000Z'; \
    if [ "${fail}" -ne 0 ]; then \
        echo "" >&2; \
        echo "       These values define WHICH COMPILED PROGRAM ARBITRATES BEHAVIOUR." >&2; \
        echo "       The compiled COBOL is the behavioural specification, so changing" >&2; \
        echo "       them changes the specification -- not just the build. Re-pin by" >&2; \
        echo "       editing harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol in a reviewable commit." >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the pinned architecture actually took effect ---'; \
    arch="$(dpkg --print-architecture)"; \
    if [ "${arch}" != 'amd64' ]; then \
        echo "FATAL: this image is being built for ${arch}, not amd64." >&2; \
        echo "       The FROM line pins --platform=linux/amd64 because COBOL COMP and" >&2; \
        echo "       BINARY-LONG storage is representation-dependent and the Python" >&2; \
        echo "       closure is an x86_64 wheel set. A foreign-architecture oracle would" >&2; \
        echo "       make a non-empty state diff an artefact of the comparison." >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo "    dpkg architecture: ${arch}   uname -m: $(uname -m)"; \
    echo 'OK: every oracle-contract pin matches the value committed in this Dockerfile.'


#  NON-INTERACTIVE, PREDICTABLE ENVIRONMENT
#  `DEBIAN_FRONTEND` is an ARG rather than an ENV on purpose: it suppresses apt
#  prompting during the BUILD without persisting into the running container,
#  where it would degrade an interactive `apt` run during investigation.
#  A UTF-8 locale is set because the compiled programs use curses screen
#  sections and the frozen schema's character-set behaviour must not vary with
#  the ambient locale. C.UTF-8 is built into glibc, so it needs no locale
#  generation and adds no variability.
#  `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE` keeps the harness Python tools from scattering
#  `__pycache__` into a bind-mounted working copy; `PYTHONUNBUFFERED` makes
#  their output interleave faithfully with the COBOL scripts' output in a
#  captured log.
ARG DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive

ENV LANG=C.UTF-8 \
    LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 \
    PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 \
    PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  SYSTEM PACKAGES -- RESOLVED FROM A FROZEN ARCHIVE SNAPSHOT, PINNED EXACTLY
#
#  Grouped by the reason each is required.
#
#  WHY THIS LAYER IS PINNED TWICE OVER. An earlier revision of this file argued
#  that the base image tag pinned the archive and that per-package pinning would
#  "fail rather than drift". Both halves of that argument were wrong, and the
#  second half had the trade backwards:
#
#    * The base image tag pins the base FILESYSTEM, not the archive. `apt-get
#      install` reaches out to `archive.ubuntu.com` and `security.ubuntu.com`,
#      which advance continuously. The same `docker build`, from the same commit,
#      a week apart, installed a different gcc, a different libgmp and a
#      different mariadb-client -- i.e. a different oracle.
#    * "Fail rather than drift" IS THE CORRECT TRADE HERE. A build failure is a
#      loud, diagnosable event that a maintainer fixes in one reviewable commit.
#      Silent drift in the arbiter corrupts the evidence instead: a non-empty
#      state diff would then be an artefact of the comparison, which AAP 0.6.6
#      forbids outright -- "a non-empty diff is always a real behavioral
#      difference and never an artefact of the comparison". Under R-6 this image
#      IS the arbiter, so its inputs must not move.
#
#  So the archive itself is pinned to `snapshot.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/${APT_SNAPSHOT}`
#  -- an immutable, cryptographically signed point-in-time view of the Ubuntu
#  archive -- AND every explicitly requested package carries an exact `=version`.
#  The snapshot freezes transitive dependencies too, which the per-package pins
#  cannot; the per-package pins make the intent auditable in the diff, which the
#  snapshot alone cannot. Neither is redundant.
#
#  `${APT_SNAPSHOT}` IS NOT A BUILD TIMESTAMP. It is a fixed archive coordinate,
#  identical on every build of this commit, so it cannot leak into any artefact
#  the harness emits and cannot perturb the byte-identical-dump guarantee that
#  the determinism suite rests on. It is gated for immutability above.
#
#  WHY THE SOURCES ARE REWRITTEN IN TWO STAGES. `ubuntu:24.04` ships no
#  `ca-certificates` package and no `/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt` at all,
#  and `snapshot.ubuntu.com` answers plain HTTP with a 301 to HTTPS -- so an apt
#  that has been repointed at the snapshot cannot fetch even the signed
#  `InRelease` until a trust store exists. Verified directly: both `http://` and
#  `https://` snapshot sources fail with "No system certificates available".
#  Stage 1 therefore installs ONLY `ca-certificates` from the stock archive,
#  whose integrity is guaranteed by the `ubuntu-archive-keyring.gpg` already
#  present in the base image. Stage 2 repoints apt at the snapshot and installs
#  the full pinned set -- re-pinning `ca-certificates`, `openssl` and
#  `libssl3t64`, the three packages stage 1 introduced, with
#  `--allow-downgrades` so that the FINAL image is entirely snapshot-derived
#  however far the live archive has since moved on.
#
#  `noble-backports` is deliberately absent from the snapshot sources. Its
#  snapshot `InRelease` fails apt's clearsign validation ("Clearsigned file isn't
#  valid, got 'NOSPLIT'"), and nothing in the package list below is served from
#  backports, so the pocket is dropped rather than worked around. `noble`,
#  `noble-updates` and `noble-security` are all present and all validate.
#
#  BUILD TOOLCHAIN -- GnuCOBOL and the connector are both source builds, and
#  the recovered C-interface rule [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.sh] invokes
#  `gcc` directly:
#      build-essential gcc make cmake autoconf automake libtool bison flex m4
#      pkg-config
#
#  GnuCOBOL DEPENDENCIES:
#      libgmp-dev      mandatory; the multiple-precision maths library
#      libdb-dev       Berkeley DB, for INDEXED (ISAM) file I/O. CRITICAL --
#                      see the GnuCOBOL layer; without it the compiled menus
#                      divert into an interactive program and the harness hangs
#      libncurses-dev  the WIDE-character ncurses headers. Required because
#                      [common/comp-common.sh:L18] compiles
#                        cobc -m accept_numeric.c -lncursesw \
#                             -A '-DHAVE_NCURSESW_NCURSES_H -DDECIMAL_RIGHT'
#                      NOTE: `libncursesw5-dev` and `libncurses5-dev` DO NOT
#                      EXIST on Ubuntu 24.04; `libncurses-dev` is the package
#                      that now ships ncursesw/ncurses.h. Verified by
#                      apt-cache policy on this base image
#      ncurses-term    the terminfo database, so a real terminal type exists at
#                      run time for the curses screen sections
#      libxml2-dev     XML GENERATE support (GnuCOBOL's default XML handler)
#      libcjson-dev    JSON GENERATE support
#
#  LINK-TIME LIBRARIES the frozen recipe names:
#      zlib1g-dev      every RDBMS-touching compile passes `-lz`
#                      [common/comp-common.sh:L26]
#      libssl-dev      required to build MariaDB Connector/C with TLS support
#
#  TRUST STORE, AND THE TWO PACKAGES THAT ARRIVE WITH IT:
#      ca-certificates the trust store apt needs to reach the snapshot at all
#      openssl         pulled in by ca-certificates in stage 1
#      libssl3t64      pulled in by openssl in stage 1
#                      All three are re-pinned in stage 2 so that no package in
#                      the final image originates from the live archive.
#
#  ARCHIVE HANDLING -- the vendored archives are unpacked INSIDE the container
#  and never modified in the checkout. `unzip` is not present in the base
#  image, so it is installed explicitly rather than assumed:
#      unzip xz-utils tar ca-certificates curl wget
#
#  RUN-TIME REQUIREMENTS of the harness scripts:
#      util-linux      provides `script`, so `run_cobol_scenario.sh` can
#                      allocate a pty for the curses menus
#      mariadb-client  `mariadb` / `mariadb-admin`, used by `reset_db.sh` and
#                      `seed.sh` to apply the frozen schema and probe readiness
#      python3         CPython 3.12.3 on this base image
#      python3-pip     installs the exactly pinned dependency set
#      python3-venv    the standard-library venv module, kept available for
#                      ad-hoc isolation without changing the default python3
#      locales         a UTF-8 locale for predictable curses behaviour
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN set -eu; \
    echo '--- stage 1: minimal trust bootstrap from the stock archive ---'; \
    echo '    (ubuntu:24.04 ships no CA store, and the snapshot host is HTTPS-only)'; \
    apt-get update; \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends ca-certificates; \
    \
    echo "--- stage 2: repointing apt at the frozen snapshot ${APT_SNAPSHOT} ---"; \
    printf '%s\n' \
        'Types: deb' \
        "URIs: https://snapshot.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/${APT_SNAPSHOT}" \
        'Suites: noble noble-updates' \
        'Components: main universe restricted multiverse' \
        'Signed-By: /usr/share/keyrings/ubuntu-archive-keyring.gpg' \
        '' \
        'Types: deb' \
        "URIs: https://snapshot.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/${APT_SNAPSHOT}" \
        'Suites: noble-security' \
        'Components: main universe restricted multiverse' \
        'Signed-By: /usr/share/keyrings/ubuntu-archive-keyring.gpg' \
        > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ubuntu.sources; \
    apt-get update 2>&1 | tee /tmp/apt-snapshot-update.log; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the snapshot pin actually took effect ---'; \
    if grep -q 'Failed to fetch' /tmp/apt-snapshot-update.log; then \
        echo "FATAL: the apt snapshot ${APT_SNAPSHOT} could not be fetched, so apt would fall back to cached or live lists and the package set would NOT be reproducible." >&2; \
        grep -m5 'Failed to fetch' /tmp/apt-snapshot-update.log >&2; \
        echo "       Check that the serial exists at https://snapshot.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/ and that the build host can reach it." >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    grep -q "snapshot.ubuntu.com/ubuntu/${APT_SNAPSHOT}" /tmp/apt-snapshot-update.log \
      || { echo 'FATAL: apt did not fetch from the snapshot endpoint; the pin was not honoured.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    rm -f /tmp/apt-snapshot-update.log; \
    \
    echo '--- installing the exactly pinned package set from the snapshot ---'; \
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends --allow-downgrades \
        autoconf=2.71-3 \
        automake=1:1.16.5-1.3ubuntu1 \
        bison=2:3.8.2+dfsg-1build2 \
        build-essential=12.10ubuntu1 \
        ca-certificates=20260601~24.04.1 \
        cmake=3.28.3-1build7 \
        curl=8.5.0-2ubuntu10.11 \
        flex=2.6.4-8.2build1 \
        gcc=4:13.2.0-7ubuntu1 \
        libcjson-dev=1.7.17-1 \
        libdb-dev=1:5.3.21ubuntu2 \
        libgmp-dev=2:6.3.0+dfsg-2ubuntu6.1 \
        libncurses-dev=6.4+20240113-1ubuntu2.1 \
        libssl-dev=3.0.13-0ubuntu3.12 \
        libssl3t64=3.0.13-0ubuntu3.12 \
        libtool=2.4.7-7build1 \
        libxml2-dev=2.9.14+dfsg-1.3ubuntu3.8 \
        locales=2.39-0ubuntu8.8 \
        m4=1.4.19-4build1 \
        make=4.3-4.1build2 \
        mariadb-client=1:10.11.14-0ubuntu0.24.04.1 \
        ncurses-term=6.4+20240113-1ubuntu2.1 \
        openssl=3.0.13-0ubuntu3.12 \
        pkg-config=1.8.1-2build1 \
        python3=3.12.3-0ubuntu2.1 \
        python3-pip=24.0+dfsg-1ubuntu1.3 \
        python3-venv=3.12.3-0ubuntu2.1 \
        tar=1.35+dfsg-3ubuntu0.4 \
        unzip=6.0-28ubuntu4.1 \
        util-linux=2.39.3-9ubuntu6.5 \
        wget=1.21.4-1ubuntu4.4 \
        xz-utils=5.6.1+really5.4.5-1ubuntu0.3 \
        zlib1g-dev=1:1.3.dfsg-3.1ubuntu2.1; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*; \
    \
    echo '--- resolved toolchain versions (build-log evidence) ---'; \
    gcc --version; \
    cmake --version; \
    python3 --version; \
    mariadb --version; \
    \
    echo '--- resolved package version set (build-log evidence for the snapshot pin) ---'; \
    dpkg-query -W -f='${Package}=${Version}\n' | sort; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the wide-character ncurses headers are present ---'; \
    test -f /usr/include/ncursesw/ncurses.h \
      || { echo 'FATAL: ncursesw/ncurses.h is absent; [common/comp-common.sh:L18] compiles accept_numeric.c with -DHAVE_NCURSESW_NCURSES_H and would fail.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the Berkeley DB header is present (ISAM backend) ---'; \
    test -f /usr/include/db.h \
      || { echo 'FATAL: db.h is absent; GnuCOBOL would configure with the indexed file handler DISABLED and the compiled menus would divert into sys002 and hang.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo "OK: system packages installed from snapshot ${APT_SNAPSHOT}; ncursesw and Berkeley DB headers verified."


#  THE MySQL CLIENT LIBRARY UNDER /usr/local/mysql -- MANDATORY, NOT OPTIONAL
#
#  Every RDBMS-touching compile in the frozen scripts hard-codes this prefix,
#  and the scripts may not be edited, so the FILESYSTEM is made to match the
#  SCRIPTS rather than the other way round:
#
#    [common/comp-common.sh:L26]  cobc -m $i cobmysqlapi.o -I ../copybooks \
#                                  -Wlinkage -L/usr/local/mysql/lib \
#                                  -lmysqlclient -lz -T $i.prn
#    [common/comp-common.sh:L32]  ... -L /usr/local/mysql/lib ...   (space)
#    [common/comp-common.sh:L34]  ... -L/usr/local/mysql/lib  ...
#    [common/comp-common.sh:L51]  cobc -x $i cobmysqlapi.o ... (the *LD loaders)
#    [general/comp-gl.sh:L2-L5]   ... -L/usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient -lz
#    [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.sh]
#                                 gcc -I/usr/local/mysql/include \
#                                     -c cobmysqlapi38.c -o cobmysqlapi.o -fPIC
#
#  So this layer MUST deliver, at those exact paths:
#      /usr/local/mysql/lib/libmysqlclient.so   (for `-lmysqlclient`)
#      /usr/local/mysql/include/mysql.h         (cobmysqlapi38.c does
#                                                `#include <mysql.h>`)
#  Note the two spellings of `-L` above -- with and without a space. Both work
#  unchanged; neither script is "normalised".
#
#  WHICH CONNECTOR, AND WHY -- MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED
#  -------------------------------------------------
#  Two vendored candidates exist and the exact-name match was tried FIRST.
#
#  REJECTED: MySQL Connector/C 6.1.11 (`mysql-connector-c-6.1.11-src.tar.gz`).
#  This is the literal `-lmysqlclient` provider and would have been preferred.
#  It was built in this exact base image and FAILS AT CMAKE CONFIGURE, before
#  any compilation:
#      -- LibMySQL 6.1.11
#      -- Built from MySQL 5.7.16 sources
#      CMake Error at cmake/install_macros.cmake:365 (ENDIF):
#        Flow control statements are not properly nested.
#      -- Configuring incomplete, errors occurred!
#  The 2017-era CMake scripts are rejected by CMake 3.28 on Ubuntu 24.04.
#  Making it build would mean PATCHING the vendored CMake scripts -- risk taken
#  on the client library for no behavioural gain. The archive is therefore not
#  even copied into the image; recording the failure here is more useful than
#  carrying 3.4 MB of unusable source in a layer.
#
#  USED: MariaDB Connector/C 3.3.4 (`mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip`). It
#  builds cleanly here and is well matched to the server, which is MariaDB
#  10.11.7 per the frozen schema dump header [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L1,L5].
#
#  This substitution is an accommodation of the BUILD HOST, never of the
#  recipe: the compiler, the client libraries and the vendored translator
#  remain exactly what the repository specifies, and no frozen script, flag or
#  path is changed. `-lmysqlclient` and `-I/usr/local/mysql/include` continue
#  to resolve verbatim [common/comp-common.sh:L26].
#
#  HOW THE MySQL-STYLE LAYOUT IS OBTAINED -- NO SYMLINK SHIM NEEDED
#  ---------------------------------------------------------------
#  MariaDB Connector/C's DEFAULT layout would install to `lib/mariadb` and
#  `include/mariadb`, which the frozen `-L.../lib` and `-I.../include` would
#  not find. Rather than paper over that with symlinks, the layout is set at
#  configure time -- `cmake/install.cmake:L112-L135` of the vendored source
#  makes INSTALL_LIBDIR, INSTALL_INCLUDEDIR, INSTALL_PLUGINDIR and
#  INSTALL_PCDIR overridable cache variables:
#      -DINSTALL_LIBDIR=lib  -DINSTALL_INCLUDEDIR=include
#      -DINSTALL_PLUGINDIR=lib/plugin
#  giving the flat, MySQL-style tree the frozen recipe expects, directly.
#
#      -DWITH_MYSQLCOMPAT=ON emits the compatibility names the frozen
#      `-lmysqlclient` requires: libmysqlclient.so -> libmariadb.so.3,
#      libmysqlclient.a -> libmariadbclient.a, plus the `_r` variants.
#      -DWITH_EXTERNAL_ZLIB=ON uses the system zlib, matching the `-lz` the
#      frozen recipe links [common/comp-common.sh:L26].
#      -DWITH_UNIT_TESTS=OFF -- the connector's own test suite needs a live
#      server and contributes nothing to the oracle.
#
#  The runtime SONAME remains `libmariadb.so.3`, which the loader resolves
#  because `/usr/local/mysql/lib` is line 3 of the repository's own loader
#  configuration [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf], reproduced verbatim below.
#
#  `make -j` here parallelises a THIRD-PARTY SOURCE BUILD only. It touches no
#  COBOL and cannot reach the compile recipe, so it does not engage the
#  no-concurrency rule (R-3), which governs scenario execution against the
#  shared database.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  SUPPLY-CHAIN INTEGRITY -- THE ARCHIVE IS VERIFIED BEFORE IT IS UNPACKED
#  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#  The archive arrives through the build CONTEXT, so it is whatever bytes sat at
#  that path when the build ran. That is a weaker guarantee than it looks:
#  nothing about a COPY authenticates the file, the client library built here is
#  linked into EVERY bridge, handler and loader of the oracle, and the oracle is
#  the arbiter of correctness for the whole migration. A substituted archive
#  would therefore compromise every answer the migration relies on, silently.
#
#  So the digest is checked FIRST and the build refuses to unpack on mismatch.
#  This is also what makes the pinned version claim meaningful: without it,
#  `MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_VERSION=3.3.4` documents an intention rather than a fact.
#
#  A SAFE-MEMBER POLICY IS APPLIED TOO, and it is deliberately belt-and-braces:
#  once the digest matches, the member list is known-good by construction. It is
#  still enforced because the two controls fail independently -- the digest can
#  only be re-pinned by a human editing this file, whereas the member policy
#  catches the case where someone re-pins to a hostile archive. It rejects
#  absolute paths, `..` traversal, symlinks and device nodes, any of which would
#  let an archive write outside its extraction directory during unpacking.
#
#  This mirrors the identical control in `harness/build_oracle.sh`, so the two
#  agree by construction rather than by convention.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ARG MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_SHA256=599b29d55fc98ea6d8263eabdf4833248ea4a8d153be862ae86ad5d220d908b0

COPY ${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP} /tmp/vendored/

# hadolint ignore=DL3003
RUN set -eu; \
    cd /tmp/vendored; \
    if [ -z "${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_SHA256}" ]; then \
        echo 'FATAL: MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_SHA256 is empty; the supply-chain check cannot be bypassed.' >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo "--- verifying ${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP} against its pinned SHA-256 ---"; \
    echo "${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_SHA256}  ${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP}" > SHA256SUMS; \
    sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP} failed its SHA-256 check; refusing to unpack an unverified archive." >&2; \
           echo "       expected ${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_SHA256}" >&2; \
           echo "       actual   $(sha256sum "${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP}" | cut -d" " -f1)" >&2; \
           echo "       This library is linked into every bridge, handler and loader of the oracle." >&2; \
           exit 1; }; \
    rm -f SHA256SUMS; \
    \
    echo '--- safe-member policy: no absolute path, no .. escape, no symlink, no device node ---'; \
    unzip -Z1 "${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP}" > /tmp/mcc-members.txt; \
    if grep -qE '^/|^([A-Za-z]:)?[\\/]' /tmp/mcc-members.txt; then \
        echo 'FATAL: the archive contains an absolute member path.' >&2; \
        grep -m5 -E '^/|^([A-Za-z]:)?[\\/]' /tmp/mcc-members.txt >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    if grep -qE '(^|/)\.\.(/|$)' /tmp/mcc-members.txt; then \
        echo 'FATAL: the archive contains a parent-directory traversal member.' >&2; \
        grep -m5 -E '(^|/)\.\.(/|$)' /tmp/mcc-members.txt >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    rm -f /tmp/mcc-members.txt; \
    \
    unzip -q "${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP}"; \
    echo '--- post-extraction policy: no symlink or device node was materialised ---'; \
    if [ -n "$(find . -type l -o -type b -o -type c -o -type p -o -type s | head -1)" ]; then \
        echo 'FATAL: the archive materialised a symlink or device node; refusing to build from it.' >&2; \
        find . -type l -o -type b -o -type c -o -type p -o -type s >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo "OK: ${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP} verified and safely unpacked."; \
    src="mariadb-connector-c-${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_VERSION}-src"; \
    test -d "${src}" \
      || { echo "FATAL: expected ${src}/ inside ${MARIADB_CONNECTOR_C_ZIP}." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    mkdir -p "${src}/build-acas"; \
    cd "${src}/build-acas"; \
    cmake .. \
        -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=RelWithDebInfo \
        -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX="${MYSQL_PREFIX}" \
        -DINSTALL_LIBDIR=lib \
        -DINSTALL_INCLUDEDIR=include \
        -DINSTALL_PLUGINDIR=lib/plugin \
        -DINSTALL_PCDIR=lib/pkgconfig \
        -DWITH_MYSQLCOMPAT=ON \
        -DWITH_EXTERNAL_ZLIB=ON \
        -DWITH_UNIT_TESTS=OFF; \
    make -j"$(nproc)"; \
    make install; \
    cd /; \
    rm -rf /tmp/vendored; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the frozen recipe resolves: -L/usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient ---'; \
    test -e "${MYSQL_PREFIX}/lib/libmysqlclient.so" \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${MYSQL_PREFIX}/lib/libmysqlclient.so is absent; every frozen compile line linking -lmysqlclient would fail. [common/comp-common.sh:L26]" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    echo '--- asserting the recovered C-interface rule resolves: -I/usr/local/mysql/include ---'; \
    test -f "${MYSQL_PREFIX}/include/mysql.h" \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${MYSQL_PREFIX}/include/mysql.h is absent; cobmysqlapi38.c does #include <mysql.h> and would fail. [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.sh]" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '--- installed client library (build-log evidence) ---'; \
    ls -l "${MYSQL_PREFIX}/lib"; \
    "${MYSQL_PREFIX}/bin/mariadb_config" --version; \
    echo 'OK: MySQL-compatible client library and headers in place under the frozen prefix.'


#  GnuCOBOL 3.2 FINAL -- BUILT FROM THE RELEASE TARBALL, NOT FROM APT
#  Distribution packages are commonly 3.1.x or 4.x, so the release tarball is
#  fetched and checked against the SHA-256 in the ARG block. Its
#  `--prefix=/usr/local` is corroborated by lines 1-2 of the repository's own
#  [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf].
#  CRITICAL: THE ISAM BACKEND -- WITHOUT IT THE ORACLE HANGS.
#  [general/general.cbl:L385-L396] moves "00" to FA-RDBMS-Flat-Statuses to
#  FORCE indexed processing of the system parameter file whatever the RDBMS
#  setting, then on a non-zero fs-reply CALLs `sys002`, which asks a human --
#  so in a harness it blocks forever. Keys 4, 2 and 1 repeat it at
#  [:L398-L417]. Hence `--with-db`; [:L462-L463] diverts the same way.
# hadolint ignore=DL3003
RUN set -eu; \
    mkdir -p /tmp/gnucobol; \
    cd /tmp/gnucobol; \
    curl -fsSL -o "${GNUCOBOL_TARBALL}" "${GNUCOBOL_URL}"; \
    echo "${GNUCOBOL_SHA256}  ${GNUCOBOL_TARBALL}" > SHA256SUMS; \
    sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${GNUCOBOL_TARBALL} failed its SHA-256 check; refusing to build an unverified arbiter." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    tar -xJf "${GNUCOBOL_TARBALL}"; \
    cd "gnucobol-${GNUCOBOL_VERSION}"; \
    grep -qx "PACKAGE_STRING='GnuCOBOL ${GNUCOBOL_VERSION}'" configure \
      || { echo "FATAL: the tarball does not self-report GnuCOBOL ${GNUCOBOL_VERSION}; a release candidate or a different release was fetched." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    ./configure \
        --prefix="${GNUCOBOL_PREFIX}" \
        --with-db \
        --with-curses=ncursesw \
        --with-math=gmp \
        --with-json=cjson \
        --with-xml2 \
        --with-dl; \
    make -j"$(nproc)"; \
    make install; \
    cd /; \
    rm -rf /tmp/gnucobol; \
    ldconfig; \
    \
    echo '--- cobc --version (build-log evidence; MUST be 3.2) ---'; \
    cobc --version > /tmp/cobc-version.txt; \
    cat /tmp/cobc-version.txt; \
    head -n 1 /tmp/cobc-version.txt > /tmp/cobc-version-line.txt; \
    grep -Eq "^cobc \\(GnuCOBOL\\) ${GNUCOBOL_VERSION}(\\.[0-9]+)*\$" /tmp/cobc-version-line.txt \
      || { echo "FATAL: cobc does not report GnuCOBOL ${GNUCOBOL_VERSION}. The oracle MUST be the compiler the maintainer targets. [common/comp-common.sh:L8-L9] [README.TXT:L53] [README.TXT:L168]" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '--- cobc --info (build-log evidence) ---'; \
    cobc --info > /tmp/cobc-info.txt 2>&1; \
    cat /tmp/cobc-info.txt; \
    grep -E 'indexed file handler' /tmp/cobc-info.txt > /tmp/cobc-isam.txt \
      || { echo 'FATAL: cobc --info reports no indexed file handler line at all.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    grep -q 'BDB' /tmp/cobc-isam.txt \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the indexed file handler is not BDB. A GnuCOBOL without ISAM makes the forced indexed OPEN at [general/general.cbl:L385-L396] fail, which CALLs the interactive sys002 and hangs the harness.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    grep -q 'GMP' /tmp/cobc-info.txt \
      || { echo 'FATAL: cobc --info does not report GMP as the mathematical library; GnuCOBOL decimal arithmetic support is missing.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    rm -f /tmp/cobc-version-line.txt /tmp/cobc-isam.txt; \
    echo "OK: GnuCOBOL ${GNUCOBOL_VERSION} final installed under ${GNUCOBOL_PREFIX} with the BDB indexed file handler."


#  RUNTIME LOADER PATHS -- THE REPOSITORY'S OWN CONFIGURATION, VERBATIM
#  [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf] is the SOLE file under `etc/`. It is four
#  lines exactly and they are reproduced below in the SAME ORDER, because
#  search order is part of the configuration:
#      /usr/local/lib/gnucobol   <- GnuCOBOL's default module directory
#      /usr/local/lib            <- libcob
#      /usr/local/mysql/lib      <- the client library (libmariadb.so.3)
#      /usr/lib                  <- the distribution's own libraries
#  The repository's copy is read and left UNTOUCHED, so it is transcribed here
#  rather than COPYed: no build step can write back to it, and this layer works
#  even when /repo is not mounted. `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` repeats the two /usr/local
#  entries as belt and braces for a module linked before any later ldconfig.
RUN set -eu; \
    printf '%s\n' \
        '/usr/local/lib/gnucobol' \
        '/usr/local/lib' \
        '/usr/local/mysql/lib' \
        '/usr/lib' \
        > /etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf; \
    ldconfig; \
    \
    echo '--- /etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf (must match [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf] exactly) ---'; \
    cat -A /etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf; \
    n_lines="$(wc -l < /etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf)"; \
    [ "${n_lines}" = '4' ] \
      || { echo "FATAL: expected exactly 4 loader path lines, found ${n_lines}. [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf]" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    n=0; \
    for expected in /usr/local/lib/gnucobol /usr/local/lib /usr/local/mysql/lib /usr/lib; do \
        n=$((n + 1)); \
        actual="$(sed -n "${n}p" /etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf)"; \
        [ "${actual}" = "${expected}" ] \
          || { echo "FATAL: loader path line ${n} is '${actual}', expected '${expected}'. The search ORDER is part of the configuration. [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf]" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    done; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting libcob and the client library both resolve ---'; \
    ldconfig -p > /tmp/ldconfig-p.txt; \
    grep -q 'libcob\.so' /tmp/ldconfig-p.txt \
      || { echo 'FATAL: libcob.so does not resolve through the loader cache.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    grep -q 'libmariadb\.so\.3' /tmp/ldconfig-p.txt \
      || { echo 'FATAL: libmariadb.so.3 (the SONAME behind libmysqlclient.so) does not resolve through the loader cache.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    rm -f /tmp/ldconfig-p.txt; \
    echo 'OK: loader paths reproduce [etc/ld.so.conf.d/gnucobol.conf] exactly, and both libraries resolve.'


#  ORACLE BOOTSTRAP STEPS 1-3 -- INCLUDING THE BUILD RULE THAT WAS MISSING
#  Steps 1-3 happen HERE and publish fixed paths: the vendored preSQL archive
#  unpacked to /opt/presql2-package, `cobmysqlapi.o` at ${ACAS_LIB_DIR}, and
#  `presql2` (plus `bldcopy2`) on the PATH. Steps 4-5 belong to
#  `build_oracle.sh` at run time, needing a live database and a writable copy.
#  STEP 2 IS THE RECOVERED RULE. Every bridge, handler and loader compile links
#  a BARE `cobmysqlapi.o` that is ABSENT from the checkout and that NO RULE IN
#  THE REPOSITORY BUILDS, so the frozen scripts alone die at link time with no
#  obvious cause. The rule comes from the archive member `cobmysqlapi38.sh`:
#      gcc -I/usr/local/mysql/include -c cobmysqlapi38.c -o cobmysqlapi.o -fPIC
#
#  `cobmysqlapi38.c` does `#include <mysql.h>` and `#include <libcob.h>`, which
#  is why this layer runs AFTER both the client library and GnuCOBOL: mysql.h
#  comes from ${MYSQL_PREFIX}/include and libcob.h from
#  ${GNUCOBOL_PREFIX}/include.
#
#  ONLY `cobmysqlapi38.c` IS USED. The package also ships two SUPERSEDED
#  variants that must NOT be used, and their own build lines show why:
#      [presql2-package/old-apis/cobmysqlapi.sh]
#          gcc -I/usr/include/mysql -c cobmysqlapi.005.c
#      [presql2-package/old-apis/cobmysqlapi3.sh]
#          gcc -I/usr/include/mysql -c cobmysqlapi3.c -o cobmysqlapi.o
#  Both use a DIFFERENT include path and both OMIT `-fPIC` -- which the frozen
#  `cobc -m` (dynamically loadable module) builds require.
#
#  The object is published at ${ACAS_LIB_DIR}/cobmysqlapi.o. `build_oracle.sh`
#  copies it into each of the six compile directories, because the frozen
#  compile lines name a BARE `cobmysqlapi.o` resolved from the current working
#  directory -- another reason those compiles cannot run inside the checkout.
#
#  STEP 3: build and install the translator, verbatim from
#  [presql2-package/presql2.sh]:
#
#      cobc -x presql2.cbl cobmysqlapi.o -L/usr/local/mysql/lib \
#           -lmysqlclient -lz
#
#  The sibling copybook utility is built too, from its own rule, so the
#  toolchain is complete rather than minimal -- [presql2-package/bldcopy2.sh]:
#
#      cobc -x bldcopy2.cbl cobmysqlapi.o -L /usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient
#
#  (note the SPACE after `-L`, and no `-lz` -- the second of the two `-L`
#  spellings that must both keep working, matching
#  [common/comp-common.sh:L32] and [:L36]).
#
#  Both translator sources take their working storage and procedure text from
#  the package's own copybooks, so all four are staged alongside them:
#      [presql2-package/presql2.cbl:L467]   COPY MYSQL-VARIABLES.
#      [presql2-package/presql2.cbl:L2685]  COPY MYSQL-PROCEDURES.
#      [presql2-package/bldcopy2.cbl:L163]  copy "mysql-variables.cpy".
#      [presql2-package/bldcopy2.cbl:L495]  copy mysql-procedures.
#  The package ships BOTH cases -- `MYSQL-VARIABLES.CPY` / `MYSQL-PROCEDURES.CPY`
#  and `mysql-variables.cpy` / `mysql-procedures.cpy` -- and they are NOT the
#  same size, so they are not duplicates to be de-duplicated. All four are
#  staged and `COBCPY` is pointed at the staging directory for this layer only.
#
#  `prtschema2` is NOT built here, and the reason is a fact about the package
#  rather than a choice: [presql2-package/prtschema2.sh] is
#
#      presql2 prtschema2.scb prtschema2.cbl
#      cobc -x prtschema2.cbl cobmysqlapi.o -L/usr/local/mysql/lib \
#           -lmysqlclient -lz
#      exit 0
#
#  and `prtschema2.cbl` DOES NOT SHIP -- the package contains only
#  `prtschema2.scb`, `prtschema2.param` and `prtschema2.sh`. The `.cbl` has to
#  be GENERATED by `presql2`, which reads a live `information_schema`, and no
#  database is reachable during an image build. It is also not needed: it is a
#  schema-printing utility, and no frozen compile script invokes it. The
#  already-generated `Variations/prtschema2M.cbl` and
#  `Variations/prtschema2o.cbl` are deliberately NOT substituted -- they are
#  different variants, and swapping one in would be an invention.
#
#  ##########################################################################
#  ##  presql2 REQUIRES A presql2.param FILE IN ITS WORKING DIRECTORY      ##
#  ##########################################################################
#  This is not documented in the compile scripts and will otherwise surprise
#  whoever runs step 4. `read_params()` in
#  [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.c:L114] builds "<progname>.param", fopen()s
#  it, and on absence prints "Could not read file >...<, aborting..." and
#  `exit(5)`. It then requires SIX cards, IN THIS ORDER, each prefix-validated
#  with `exit(6)` on mismatch and each truncated to 32 characters:
#
#      DBHOST=<host>
#      DBUSER=<user>
#      DBPASSWD=<password>
#      DBNAME=<schema>
#      DBPORT=<port>
#      DBSOCKET=<unix socket path, may be empty for TCP>
#
#  NO `.param` file exists anywhere in the checkout, and none is created here:
#  baking one into an image layer would bake in a credential. The placeholders
#  above are the SHAPE only. `harness/build_oracle.sh` materialises the file at
#  run time from ACAS_DB_HOST / ACAS_DB_USER / ACAS_DB_PASSWORD / ACAS_DB_NAME
#  / ACAS_DB_PORT / ACAS_DB_SOCKET, in the working directory where `presql2`
#  runs, OUTSIDE the checkout. Because `presql2` opens a live connection, step 4
#  of the bootstrap additionally requires the `mariadb` service to be healthy.
#  The vendored `presql2-package/presql2.param` is left in place unread as
#  package documentation; it carries example credential literals and must never
#  be copied into a working directory.
#
#  Nothing in this layer is a `cobc` flag change: every command above is
#  transcribed from a vendored `.sh` file (R-4).
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  SUPPLY-CHAIN INTEGRITY -- VERIFIED BEFORE UNPACKING, AND THE DIGEST IS SHARED
#  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  This archive is the single most security-relevant input in the whole harness.
#  It supplies BOTH the `presql2` translator that rewrites every `common/*MT.scb`
#  into compilable COBOL AND `cobmysqlapi38.c`, the recovered C interface object
#  that every bridge, handler and loader links. A substituted archive could
#  therefore alter the generated bridges and the SQL the oracle actually issues
#  -- which is to say it could change the answers the migration treats as
#  ground truth, with no diff anywhere in the repository to reveal it.
#
#  ==> THE DIGEST BELOW MUST STAY EQUAL TO THE ONE IN harness/build_oracle.sh. <==
#
#  That is not a stylistic preference. `build_oracle.sh` verifies the same file
#  with its own pinned digest and its diagnostics tell the operator that the two
#  declarations must agree; if they diverged, that diagnostic would be a lie and
#  an operator debugging a mismatch would be sent in the wrong direction. Change
#  one only by changing both, in the same commit.
#
#  As with the connector archive, a safe-member policy is applied on top of the
#  digest: absolute paths, `..` traversal, symlinks and device nodes are all
#  rejected. The digest makes the member list known-good, but the two controls
#  fail independently and the policy is what catches a re-pin to a hostile
#  archive.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ARG PRESQL2_SHA256=638db9530d2fe008fbb46cf8600b9406f6f780c9fc59d0e0c94e868b4d615475

COPY ${PRESQL2_ZIP} /tmp/presql2/

# hadolint ignore=DL3003
RUN set -eu; \
    cd /tmp/presql2; \
    if [ -z "${PRESQL2_SHA256}" ]; then \
        echo 'FATAL: PRESQL2_SHA256 is empty; the supply-chain check cannot be bypassed.' >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo "--- verifying ${PRESQL2_ZIP} against its pinned SHA-256 ---"; \
    echo "${PRESQL2_SHA256}  ${PRESQL2_ZIP}" > SHA256SUMS; \
    sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${PRESQL2_ZIP} failed its SHA-256 check; refusing to unpack an unverified archive." >&2; \
           echo "       expected ${PRESQL2_SHA256}" >&2; \
           echo "       actual   $(sha256sum "${PRESQL2_ZIP}" | cut -d" " -f1)" >&2; \
           echo "       This archive supplies presql2 (which rewrites every common/*MT.scb) and" >&2; \
           echo "       cobmysqlapi38.c (which every bridge, handler and loader links)." >&2; \
           echo "       The same digest is pinned in harness/build_oracle.sh; the two MUST agree." >&2; \
           exit 1; }; \
    rm -f SHA256SUMS; \
    \
    echo '--- safe-member policy: no absolute path, no .. escape, no symlink, no device node ---'; \
    unzip -Z1 "${PRESQL2_ZIP}" > /tmp/presql2-members.txt; \
    if grep -qE '^/|^([A-Za-z]:)?[\\/]' /tmp/presql2-members.txt; then \
        echo 'FATAL: the archive contains an absolute member path.' >&2; \
        grep -m5 -E '^/|^([A-Za-z]:)?[\\/]' /tmp/presql2-members.txt >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    if grep -qE '(^|/)\.\.(/|$)' /tmp/presql2-members.txt; then \
        echo 'FATAL: the archive contains a parent-directory traversal member.' >&2; \
        grep -m5 -E '(^|/)\.\.(/|$)' /tmp/presql2-members.txt >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    rm -f /tmp/presql2-members.txt; \
    \
    unzip -q "${PRESQL2_ZIP}"; \
    echo '--- post-extraction policy: no symlink or device node was materialised ---'; \
    if [ -n "$(find . -type l -o -type b -o -type c -o -type p -o -type s | head -1)" ]; then \
        echo 'FATAL: the archive materialised a symlink or device node; refusing to build from it.' >&2; \
        find . -type l -o -type b -o -type c -o -type p -o -type s >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo "OK: ${PRESQL2_ZIP} verified and safely unpacked."; \
    test -d presql2-package \
      || { echo "FATAL: expected presql2-package/ inside ${PRESQL2_ZIP}." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    mkdir -p /opt; \
    mv presql2-package /opt/presql2-package; \
    cd /; \
    rm -rf /tmp/presql2; \
    \
    echo "--- JC preSQL package unpacked (package version ${PRESQL2_PACKAGE_VERSION}, program version ${PRESQL2_PROGRAM_VERSION}) ---"; \
    find /opt/presql2-package -mindepth 1 > /tmp/presql2-entries.txt; \
    n_entries="$(wc -l < /tmp/presql2-entries.txt)"; \
    rm -f /tmp/presql2-entries.txt; \
    echo "entries under /opt/presql2-package: ${n_entries}"; \
    for required in cobmysqlapi38.c cobmysqlapi38.sh presql2.cbl presql2.sh \
                    bldcopy2.cbl bldcopy2.sh \
                    MYSQL-VARIABLES.CPY MYSQL-PROCEDURES.CPY \
                    mysql-variables.cpy mysql-procedures.cpy; do \
        test -f "/opt/presql2-package/${required}" \
          || { echo "FATAL: /opt/presql2-package/${required} is missing from the vendored archive." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    done; \
    test -f /opt/presql2-package/old-apis/cobmysqlapi.005.c \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the vendored archive layout is not as expected; old-apis/ should exist and be left unused.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    test -d /opt/presql2-package/ACAS \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the vendored worked ACAS example is missing; the end-to-end toolchain smoke test depends on it.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    mkdir -p "${ACAS_LIB_DIR}"; \
    build=/tmp/acas-bootstrap; \
    mkdir -p "${build}"; \
    cp -p /opt/presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.c \
          /opt/presql2-package/presql2.cbl \
          /opt/presql2-package/bldcopy2.cbl \
          /opt/presql2-package/MYSQL-VARIABLES.CPY \
          /opt/presql2-package/MYSQL-PROCEDURES.CPY \
          /opt/presql2-package/mysql-variables.cpy \
          /opt/presql2-package/mysql-procedures.cpy \
          "${build}/"; \
    cd "${build}"; \
    COBCPY="${build}"; export COBCPY; \
    COB_COPY_DIR="${build}"; export COB_COPY_DIR; \
    \
    echo '--- STEP 2: the recovered rule, verbatim [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.sh] ---'; \
    gcc -I/usr/local/mysql/include -c cobmysqlapi38.c -o cobmysqlapi.o -fPIC; \
    install -m 0644 cobmysqlapi.o "${ACAS_LIB_DIR}/cobmysqlapi.o"; \
    \
    echo '--- STEP 3: the translator, verbatim [presql2-package/presql2.sh] ---'; \
    cobc -x presql2.cbl cobmysqlapi.o -L/usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient -lz; \
    install -m 0755 presql2 /usr/local/bin/presql2; \
    \
    echo '--- STEP 3 (sibling): [presql2-package/bldcopy2.sh] -- note the space after -L ---'; \
    cobc -x bldcopy2.cbl cobmysqlapi.o -L /usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient; \
    install -m 0755 bldcopy2 /usr/local/bin/bldcopy2; \
    \
    cd /; \
    rm -rf "${build}"; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the published bootstrap artifacts ---'; \
    test -f "${ACAS_LIB_DIR}/cobmysqlapi.o" \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${ACAS_LIB_DIR}/cobmysqlapi.o was not published; every frozen bridge, handler and loader compile links it. [common/comp-common.sh:L26]" >&2; exit 1; }; \
    command -v presql2 > /dev/null \
      || { echo 'FATAL: presql2 is not on the PATH; [common/comp-common.sh:L25] invokes it to translate every *MT.scb.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    command -v bldcopy2 > /dev/null \
      || { echo 'FATAL: bldcopy2 is not on the PATH.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '--- linkage evidence: presql2 resolves libcob and the client library ---'; \
    ldd /usr/local/bin/presql2 > /tmp/presql2-ldd.txt; \
    cat /tmp/presql2-ldd.txt; \
    if grep -q 'not found' /tmp/presql2-ldd.txt; then \
        echo 'FATAL: presql2 has unresolved shared-library dependencies.' >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    rm -f /tmp/presql2-ldd.txt; \
    echo "OK: bootstrap steps 1-3 complete -- /opt/presql2-package, ${ACAS_LIB_DIR}/cobmysqlapi.o, /usr/local/bin/{presql2,bldcopy2}."


#  END-TO-END TOOLCHAIN SELF-CHECK -- THE VENDORED WORKED ACAS EXAMPLE
#  This exercises the whole chain -- GnuCOBOL 3.2, the client library under the
#  frozen prefix, and the recovered `cobmysqlapi.o` -- before the frozen tree
#  is touched, by compiling a real generated ACAS bridge with the maintainer's
#  own line, verbatim from archive member `comp-stockMT.sh`: `cobc -m
#  stockMT.COB cobmysqlapi.o -L/usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient -lz`.
#  Its `presql2` line is commented out in the package and is not run here
#  either, so this needs NO database: `cobc -m` compiles and links only. It
#  uses the VENDORED example, never the checkout -- the frozen `common/*MT.cbl`
#  are not compiled and the scratch directory is removed. A second guard
#  compiles and runs a throwaway INDEXED-file program; ISAM failure aborts.
# hadolint ignore=DL3003
RUN set -eu; \
    smoke=/tmp/acas-smoke; \
    rm -rf "${smoke}"; \
    cp -a /opt/presql2-package/ACAS "${smoke}"; \
    cd "${smoke}"; \
    cp -p "${ACAS_LIB_DIR}/cobmysqlapi.o" ./cobmysqlapi.o; \
    \
    echo '--- reproducing [presql2-package/ACAS/comp-stockMT.sh] verbatim ---'; \
    cobc -m stockMT.COB cobmysqlapi.o -L/usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient -lz; \
    test -f stockMT.so \
      || { echo 'FATAL: stockMT.so was not produced. The COBOL-to-MySQL toolchain is incomplete; every frozen bridge compile would fail the same way. [presql2-package/ACAS/comp-stockMT.sh]' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    ldd stockMT.so > ./stockMT.ldd; \
    cat ./stockMT.ldd; \
    if grep -q 'not found' ./stockMT.ldd; then \
        echo 'FATAL: stockMT.so has unresolved shared-library dependencies; -L/usr/local/mysql/lib -lmysqlclient did not resolve at run time.' >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo 'OK: a real generated ACAS bridge compiles and links against the recovered cobmysqlapi.o.'; \
    \
    echo '--- proving the BDB indexed handler actually works, not just that it is configured ---'; \
    printf '%s\n' \
        '       identification division.' \
        '       program-id. blitzyisamprobe.' \
        '       environment division.' \
        '       input-output section.' \
        '       file-control.' \
        '           select probe-file assign to "isam-probe"' \
        '               organization indexed' \
        '               access mode dynamic' \
        '               record key probe-key' \
        '               file status probe-status.' \
        '       data division.' \
        '       file section.' \
        '       fd  probe-file.' \
        '       01  probe-rec.' \
        '           03  probe-key   pic x(4).' \
        '           03  probe-data  pic x(8).' \
        '       working-storage section.' \
        '       01  probe-status    pic xx value spaces.' \
        '       procedure division.' \
        '           open output probe-file.' \
        '           if probe-status not = "00"' \
        '               display "ISAM-OPEN-OUTPUT-FAILED " probe-status' \
        '               stop run returning 1' \
        '           end-if.' \
        '           move "K001" to probe-key.' \
        '           move "PAYLOAD1" to probe-data.' \
        '           write probe-rec.' \
        '           if probe-status not = "00"' \
        '               display "ISAM-WRITE-FAILED " probe-status' \
        '               stop run returning 1' \
        '           end-if.' \
        '           close probe-file.' \
        '           open input probe-file.' \
        '           move "K001" to probe-key.' \
        '           read probe-file key is probe-key.' \
        '           if probe-status not = "00" or probe-data not = "PAYLOAD1"' \
        '               display "ISAM-READ-FAILED " probe-status " " probe-data' \
        '               stop run returning 1' \
        '           end-if.' \
        '           close probe-file.' \
        '           display "ISAM-OK".' \
        '           stop run returning 0.' \
        > blitzyisamprobe.cbl; \
    cobc -x blitzyisamprobe.cbl; \
    ./blitzyisamprobe > ./isam.out 2>&1 \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the indexed-file probe failed to run.' >&2; cat ./isam.out; exit 1; }; \
    cat ./isam.out; \
    grep -qx 'ISAM-OK' ./isam.out \
      || { echo 'FATAL: INDEXED file I/O does not work. [general/general.cbl:L385-L396] forces COBOL indexed processing for the system parameter file and, on failure, CALLs the interactive sys002 -- which hangs a harness forever.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    cd /; \
    rm -rf "${smoke}"; \
    test ! -e /tmp/acas-smoke \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the smoke-test scratch directory was not removed; no build artifact may persist into the image.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    echo 'OK: end-to-end toolchain proven, and INDEXED file I/O verified working.'


#  THE PYTHON SIDE -- EXACTLY THE REPOSITORY'S PINS, AND NOTHING ELSE
#
#  `requirements.txt` is copied from the build context (the repository root)
#  and installed as-is. It carries twelve `name==version` lines and no ranges,
#  no extras, no index URLs, no editable or VCS requirements, and no hashes --
#  so `--require-hashes` is NOT used, because inventing hashes would break
#  installation. The pins are the same ones `pyproject.toml` declares; a
#  mismatch between the two manifests is a defect, so neither is edited here.
#
#  `--break-system-packages` is required because Ubuntu 24.04 marks its system
#  Python PEP-668 externally-managed. Installing into the SYSTEM interpreter is
#  deliberate rather than lazy: it keeps `python3` the Ubuntu-provided CPython
#  3.12.3 that the plan names as the target runtime, with no venv indirection
#  for the harness scripts to activate. The container is single-purpose and
#  disposable, so there is no host environment to protect.
#
#  ##########################################################################
#  ##  R-2 SELF-CHECK: ZERO BINARY FLOATING POINT                          ##
#  ##########################################################################
#  No accounting value may pass through a binary floating-point type at any
#  point -- not in computation, not in storage, not in transport. The exclusion
#  of `pandas` and `numpy` is ABSOLUTE and extends to the harness dump
#  comparison, which uses ordered row sequences rather than dataframes. The
#  assertions below prove BOTH are absent, so a transitive dependency cannot
#  smuggle either in without failing this build.
#
#  The interpreter's `decimal` must be the C libmpdec implementation, not the
#  pure-Python fallback -- every monetary and quantity operation in the
#  migrated cycle passes through it, so this matters for correctness as well as
#  speed. `import _decimal` succeeding is the check for that.
#
#  ALSO DELIBERATELY ABSENT, each for a stated reason:
#      pytest-xdist and every other parallel plugin -- execution is strictly
#          sequential, matching the single-threaded COBOL, and parallel
#          scenario runs against one shared MariaDB would destroy the
#          seed/run/dump/reset/run/dump/diff protocol
#      jsonschema -- not in `requirements.txt`. Everything under `harness/`
#          works with the standard library plus PyYAML plus the driver
#      any ORM entity layer, alembic and every migration tool -- the schema is
#          frozen and no DDL is ever emitted
#      any asyncio / threading / connection-pool helper
#      any third-party date library -- `acas_posting/dates.py` reproduces
#          [common/maps04.cbl] exactly, defects included, and a general-purpose
#          date library would be MORE CORRECT THAN THE SPECIFICATION
#
#  The `acas_posting` package is NOT installed into this image. Nothing here
#  puts `harness/` on `PYTHONPATH` or makes it importable (R-1);
#  `run_python_scenario.sh` runs `python -m acas_posting ...` against the
#  repository working copy, and the harness tools are invoked by path.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  ALL THREE LOCK FILES, AND WHY
#  ----------------------------
#  The Python side is locked across THREE files, and this image needs every one
#  of them:
#
#      requirements.txt          the SHIPPED runtime: the database driver,
#                                SQLAlchemy (Core only) and their transitive
#                                pins
#      requirements-harness.txt  PyYAML -- read by nothing in `acas_posting`,
#                                but required to parse harness/scenarios/*.yaml
#      requirements-dev.txt      pytest and pytest-cov, which run the arithmetic,
#                                scenario and determinism suites, plus their
#                                transitive pins
#
#  Installing only the first would break this image outright: the assertions
#  below import `yaml` and `pytest`, which live in the second and third files.
#  The split exists so the SHIPPED artifact does not carry a test runner, and
#  this image is where all three sets legitimately come together -- it is the
#  one place that both runs the harness and executes the suites.
#
#  --require-hashes -- EVERY ARTIFACT IS AUTHENTICATED
#  --------------------------------------------------
#  A version pin (`name==1.2.3`) states WHICH release is wanted; it does not
#  prove the bytes that arrive are that release. `--require-hashes` closes that
#  gap: pip refuses to install any artifact whose SHA-256 is not declared in the
#  lock file, so a compromised index, a poisoned cache or a hijacked mirror
#  fails the build instead of silently substituting code into the arbiter.
#
#  It also makes the lock files self-enforcing in a way worth stating: under
#  `--require-hashes` pip requires EVERY requirement, including every transitive
#  one, to be pinned and hashed. An incomplete lock therefore cannot be
#  installed at all rather than quietly resolving the gap at build time. The
#  three files are complete and mutually disjoint, verified.
#
#  All three are passed to a SINGLE pip invocation so the resolver sees the
#  union once and cannot satisfy one file's dependency with a different version
#  than another's.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
COPY requirements.txt requirements-harness.txt requirements-dev.txt /opt/acas-harness/

RUN set -eu; \
    echo '--- installing the locked Python set, every artifact hash-verified ---'; \
    python3 -m pip install --no-cache-dir --break-system-packages --require-hashes \
        -r /opt/acas-harness/requirements.txt \
        -r /opt/acas-harness/requirements-harness.txt \
        -r /opt/acas-harness/requirements-dev.txt; \
    \
    echo '--- interpreter identity (must be CPython 3.12.x) ---'; \
    python3 --version; \
    python3 -c 'import sys; assert sys.version_info[:2] == (3, 12), f"FATAL: expected CPython 3.12.x, found {sys.version.split()[0]}"'; \
    \
    echo '--- decimal must be the C libmpdec implementation ---'; \
    python3 -c 'import decimal, _decimal; assert decimal.HAVE_THREADS is not None; print("decimal backed by:", _decimal.__name__)'; \
    \
    echo '--- truncation semantics: an un-ROUNDED COBOL store discards, it does not round ---'; \
    python3 -c 'from decimal import Decimal, ROUND_DOWN; q = Decimal("1.239").quantize(Decimal("0.01"), rounding=ROUND_DOWN); assert q == Decimal("1.23"), q; print("ROUND_DOWN 1.239 ->", q)'; \
    \
    echo '--- the pinned set must import ---'; \
    python3 -c 'import mysql.connector, sqlalchemy, yaml, pytest; print("mysql-connector-python", mysql.connector.__version__); print("SQLAlchemy", sqlalchemy.__version__); print("PyYAML", yaml.__version__); print("pytest", pytest.__version__)'; \
    \
    echo '--- R-2 self-check: pandas and numpy MUST be absent ---'; \
    if python3 -c 'import pandas' > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo 'FATAL: pandas is installed. Zero binary floating point in accounting computation -- the exclusion is absolute and extends to the harness dump comparison.' >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    if python3 -c 'import numpy' > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo 'FATAL: numpy is installed. Zero binary floating point in accounting computation -- the exclusion is absolute and extends to the harness dump comparison.' >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo 'OK: pandas and numpy are both absent.'; \
    \
    echo '--- no-concurrency self-check: no parallel test plugin may be present ---'; \
    if python3 -c 'import xdist' > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo 'FATAL: pytest-xdist is installed. Execution must be strictly sequential; parallel scenario runs against one shared database would break the seed/run/dump/reset/run/dump/diff protocol.' >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo 'OK: no parallel test plugin present.'; \
    \
    echo '--- no schema-migration tool may be present ---'; \
    if python3 -c 'import alembic' > /dev/null 2>&1; then \
        echo 'FATAL: alembic is installed. mysql/ACASDB.sql is frozen and no DDL is ever emitted.' >&2; exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo 'OK: no schema-migration tool present.'; \
    \
    echo '--- installed distributions (build-log evidence) ---'; \
    python3 -m pip list --format=freeze; \
    echo 'OK: the Python side is exactly the repository pin set.'


#  MOUNT POINTS -- THE READ-ONLY / WRITABLE SPLIT THE FROZEN TREE DEPENDS ON
#      /repo   the checkout, mounted READ-ONLY. The frozen COBOL, copybooks,
#              bridges and `mysql/ACASDB.sql` are read in place from here.
#      /build  a WRITABLE copy, made by `build_oracle.sh` before it runs the
#              maintainer's compile scripts. Everything the scripts create --
#              regenerated `*MT.cbl`, `.prn` listings, six copies of
#              `cobmysqlapi.o`, `presql2.param`, `.so` modules and executables
#              -- lands here and never in the checkout.
#      /data   the ledger data directory: COBOL indexed files and the flat seed
#              files the loaders read.
#      /out    dumps, normalised dumps and diff evidence.
#  /build/bin exists so that ACAS_BIN resolves; see the environment block.
#
#  THESE ARE EMPTY MOUNT POINTS, AND /build IS EMPTY UNTIL build_oracle.sh RUNS.
#  This layer creates the four directories and nothing else. It deliberately does
#  NOT pre-populate /build with a copy of the tree: the copy is made at run time
#  from the read-only mount, so that what gets compiled is the checkout the
#  operator actually mounted rather than whatever was baked into the image (R-6 --
#  the arbiter must correspond to the commit under test, not to the build date).
#
#  ONE CONSEQUENCE MUST BE STATED HERE, BECAUSE IT IS EASY TO GET WRONG. WORKDIR
#  is /build, so a RELATIVE invocation such as
#      docker compose run --rm -T gnucobol harness/build_oracle.sh
#  resolves to /build/harness/build_oracle.sh -- which does not exist on a fresh
#  stack, nor after `docker compose down -v`, because /build is an empty named
#  volume until the bootstrap copies the tree into it. The very first command of
#  the protocol would fail with a file-not-found, before any script could explain
#  why. EVERY harness script is therefore invoked BY ABSOLUTE PATH from the
#  read-only mount, `/repo/harness/<script>`, and the canonical invocations in
#  [harness/docker-compose.yml] are written that way. `build_oracle.sh` prescribes
#  the same fix itself when its re-exec guard cannot recover: "Invoke
#  $ACAS_REPO/harness/build_oracle.sh directly."
#
#  UNPRIVILEGED RUNTIME IDENTITY -- `acas`, uid 1001, gid 1001
#
#  This container is not a short-lived build step. The compose service idles and
#  is exec'd into repeatedly: `build_oracle.sh`, then `seed.sh`, then a scenario
#  run, then `dump_tables.py`, then `reset_db.sh`, then the Python side, then the
#  diff -- a long-lived process, driving a compiled COBOL toolchain over data it
#  did not author. Running all of that as root buys nothing and costs the usual:
#  every write it makes lands root-owned in the `/data` and `/out` volumes the
#  host shares, and any defect in the compiled programs -- which, per R-4, are
#  reproduced WITH their defects and explicitly not hardened -- executes with
#  full privilege.
#
#  So the image installs everything as root at BUILD time and drops to `acas`
#  for RUN time. The split falls exactly where it should:
#
#      root-owned, world-readable, NOT writable by `acas`
#          /usr/local/bin/{cobc,presql2,bldcopy2}   the toolchain
#          /usr/local/lib/gnucobol, /usr/local/mysql the runtime libraries
#          /usr/local/lib/acas/cobmysqlapi.o        read and copied, never rewritten
#          /opt/presql2-package                     the unpacked vendored archive
#          /repo                                    THE FROZEN CHECKOUT
#
#      acas-owned, writable
#          /build      the writable copy the compile scripts work in
#          /build/bin  so ACAS_BIN resolves
#          /data       ledger data and the flat seed files
#          /out        dumps, normalised dumps, diff evidence, build logs
#          /home/acas  a real home, so relative-path artefacts have somewhere to
#                      go and nothing falls back to /root
#
#  `/repo` STAYING ROOT-OWNED IS A FEATURE, NOT AN OVERSIGHT. It reinforces the
#  read-only mount: even if the `:ro` flag were ever dropped from the compose
#  file by accident, the runtime user still could not write the frozen tree.
#  Defence in depth over exactly the guarantee this harness exists to protect.
#
#  THE NUMERIC IDS ARE FIXED DELIBERATELY. `/data` and `/out` are shared with the
#  host, so file ownership must be predictable across rebuilds rather than
#  whatever `useradd` happens to pick next. 1001:1001 is the DEFAULT because
#  `ubuntu:24.04` already ships a `ubuntu` account at 1000:1000 - and overriding
#  the arguments to 1000:1000, which `--build-arg ACAS_UID=$(id -u)` produces on a
#  first-user host, is now handled explicitly rather than failing the build: the
#  layer below renames that base-image account to `ubuntu-base` and takes the id.
#  Nothing in this image refers to it (m-02).
#
#  THE HARNESS SCRIPTS ALREADY ASSUME THIS. They were written not to require
#  root and say so: `seed.sh` notes that "ldconfig needs privileges this script
#  does not assume" and relies on LD_LIBRARY_PATH instead; `build_oracle.sh`
#  treats a failed `ldconfig` as a warning rather than fatal; and its step 3
#  install into /usr/local/bin is reached only when `presql2` is absent from the
#  PATH -- which cannot happen in this image, because the build published it.
#  Dropping privilege therefore changes no script and removes no capability.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  OWNERSHIP -- the four writable paths are handed to the unprivileged runtime
#  account created in the next layer, so the harness never needs root at run
#  time. /repo stays root-owned: it is a READ-ONLY mount point and the runtime
#  account must not be able to write to it even if the `:ro` flag were ever
#  dropped by mistake. That is defence in depth for the frozen-artifact
#  guarantee, and it costs nothing.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ARG ACAS_UID=1001
ARG ACAS_GID=1001
ARG ACAS_USER=acas

#  ⭐ m-02.  ONE IDENTITY, CREATED ONCE, FROM THE BUILD ARGUMENTS.
#
#  This layer used to hard-code `1001` and `acas` while the ARGs above declared
#  `ACAS_UID`/`ACAS_GID`/`ACAS_USER`, so a build that overrode them got an account
#  created HERE at 1001:1001 and then RECONCILED by a second `groupmod`/`usermod`
#  layer further down - two creations, two sources of truth, and a window in which
#  the ownership assertions below described a different identity from the one the
#  image finally ran as. Everything now derives from the arguments, and the
#  reconciliation layer below verifies rather than re-creates.
#
#  AN OCCUPIED UID OR GID IS HANDLED EXPLICITLY, which is the other half of the
#  finding. The base image ships `ubuntu` at 1000:1000, so
#  `--build-arg ACAS_UID=1000 --build-arg ACAS_GID=1000` - exactly what the
#  documented `$(id -u)` invocation produces on a first-user host - made
#  `groupadd`/`useradd` fail with "GID 1000 already in use" and, under `set -eu`,
#  aborted the build with no explanation. The base image's own account is now
#  REMOVED when it holds a wanted ID, because it exists only as a distribution
#  convenience and nothing in this image refers to it, and its removal makes the
#  creation path below identical in both cases - which is the "created once" the
#  finding asks for. A conflict with any OTHER account is refused loudly instead
#  of silently taking its ID.
#
#  AN ID IS FREED BY REMOVING ITS HOLDER, NEVER BY RENAMING IT. `groupmod
#  --new-name` and `usermod --login` change the NAME and leave the numeric ID
#  exactly where it was, so a rename followed by `groupadd --gid 1000` still fails
#  with "GID '1000' already exists". Measured directly against this image's own
#  account database rather than assumed, because the reverse assumption is the
#  natural one and it is wrong.
#
#  AND: the RUN body below is parsed by `/bin/sh` - this file declares no `SHELL`
#  directive, so Docker uses `/bin/sh -c`, which on Ubuntu is dash. It must
#  therefore stay POSIX shell, and an apostrophe inside a DOUBLE-quoted string is
#  written plainly: the `'"'"'` idiom belongs only inside SINGLE quotes, and using
#  it here leaves an odd number of single quotes and an unparsable layer.
RUN set -eu; \
    echo "--- creating the unprivileged runtime identity ${ACAS_USER} (${ACAS_UID}:${ACAS_GID}) ---"; \
    if [ "${ACAS_UID}" = '0' ] || [ "${ACAS_GID}" = '0' ]; then \
        echo 'FATAL: ACAS_UID/ACAS_GID must not be 0; the point of this identity is to drop root.' >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    if [ "$(getent passwd "${ACAS_UID}" | cut -d: -f1)" = 'ubuntu' ] \
       || [ "$(getent group "${ACAS_GID}" | cut -d: -f1)" = 'ubuntu' ]; then \
        echo "    ${ACAS_UID}:${ACAS_GID} overlaps the base image's own 'ubuntu' account; removing it"; \
        if getent passwd ubuntu > /dev/null; then \
            userdel -r ubuntu 2> /dev/null || userdel ubuntu 2> /dev/null || true; \
        fi; \
        if getent passwd ubuntu > /dev/null; then \
            echo "FATAL: could not remove the base image's 'ubuntu' account, which holds ${ACAS_UID}." >&2; \
            exit 1; \
        fi; \
        if getent group ubuntu > /dev/null; then groupdel ubuntu 2> /dev/null || true; fi; \
        if getent group ubuntu > /dev/null; then \
            echo "FATAL: could not remove the base image's 'ubuntu' group, which holds ${ACAS_GID}." >&2; \
            exit 1; \
        fi; \
    fi; \
    occupant="$(getent group "${ACAS_GID}" | cut -d: -f1)"; \
    if [ -n "${occupant}" ] && [ "${occupant}" != "${ACAS_USER}" ]; then \
        echo "FATAL: gid ${ACAS_GID} is already held by group '${occupant}'. Choose another ACAS_GID." >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    occupant="$(getent passwd "${ACAS_UID}" | cut -d: -f1)"; \
    if [ -n "${occupant}" ] && [ "${occupant}" != "${ACAS_USER}" ]; then \
        echo "FATAL: uid ${ACAS_UID} is already held by account '${occupant}'. Choose another ACAS_UID." >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    getent group "${ACAS_USER}" > /dev/null \
      || groupadd --gid "${ACAS_GID}" "${ACAS_USER}"; \
    getent passwd "${ACAS_USER}" > /dev/null \
      || useradd --uid "${ACAS_UID}" --gid "${ACAS_GID}" --create-home \
                 --home-dir "/home/${ACAS_USER}" \
                 --shell /bin/bash \
                 --comment 'ACAS posting-cycle oracle harness' "${ACAS_USER}"; \
    \
    mkdir -p /repo /build /build/bin /data /out "/home/${ACAS_USER}"; \
    chmod 0755 /repo /build /build/bin /data /out "/home/${ACAS_USER}"; \
    chown root:root /repo; \
    chown -R "${ACAS_USER}:${ACAS_USER}" /build /data /out "/home/${ACAS_USER}"; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the ownership split ---'; \
    for d in /build /build/bin /data /out "/home/${ACAS_USER}"; do \
        owner="$(stat -c '%U:%G' "$d")"; \
        [ "${owner}" = "${ACAS_USER}:${ACAS_USER}" ] \
          || { echo "FATAL: ${d} is owned by ${owner}, not ${ACAS_USER}:${ACAS_USER}; the unprivileged runtime could not write it." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    done; \
    repo_owner="$(stat -c '%U' /repo)"; \
    [ "${repo_owner}" = 'root' ] \
      || { echo "FATAL: /repo is owned by ${repo_owner}, not root; the frozen checkout must not be writable by the runtime user." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo "--- asserting ${ACAS_USER} can actually write every writable path ---"; \
    su -s /bin/sh -c 'set -eu; for d in /build /build/bin /data /out "$HOME"; do probe="$d/.acas-write-probe"; : > "$probe"; rm -f "$probe"; done' "${ACAS_USER}" \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${ACAS_USER} cannot write one of /build /build/bin /data /out /home/${ACAS_USER}." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo "--- asserting ${ACAS_USER} CANNOT write the frozen mount point ---"; \
    if su -s /bin/sh -c ': > /repo/.acas-write-probe' "${ACAS_USER}" 2>/dev/null; then \
        rm -f /repo/.acas-write-probe; \
        echo "FATAL: ${ACAS_USER} can write /repo. The frozen tree must be unwritable by the runtime user." >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    \
    echo "--- asserting the identity IS the one the build arguments asked for ---"; \
    [ "$(id -u "${ACAS_USER}")" = "${ACAS_UID}" ] \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${ACAS_USER} has uid $(id -u "${ACAS_USER}"), not ACAS_UID=${ACAS_UID}." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    [ "$(id -g "${ACAS_USER}")" = "${ACAS_GID}" ] \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${ACAS_USER} has gid $(id -g "${ACAS_USER}"), not ACAS_GID=${ACAS_GID}." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    id "${ACAS_USER}"; \
    echo "OK: /repo root-owned; /build, /build/bin, /data, /out and /home/${ACAS_USER} owned by ${ACAS_USER}:${ACAS_USER} (${ACAS_UID}:${ACAS_GID})."


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  DROP ROOT -- THE CONTAINER RUNS AS AN UNPRIVILEGED ACCOUNT
#
#  Nothing this image does at RUN time needs root, and that is a MEASURED claim
#  rather than an assumption. Everything that would have needed it is already
#  done above, at BUILD time, and `harness/build_oracle.sh` detects and reuses
#  the results instead of rebuilding them:
#
#    * `/usr/local/lib/acas/cobmysqlapi.o` is installed by the bootstrap layer,
#      and step 2 of the build script takes the branch
#      `if [[ -s "$published" ]]` -> "reusing the object
#      harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol already built", skipping its own compile and
#      its own write under /usr/local.
#    * `/usr/local/bin/presql2` (and `bldcopy2`) are installed by the same
#      layer, and step 3 takes the branch `if acas_have presql2` ->
#      "presql2 is already on the PATH", skipping the `install ...
#      /usr/local/bin/presql2` that would otherwise require root.
#
#  Verified by inspection of all four harness shell scripts: the only remaining
#  runtime writes are to /build, /data, /out and /tmp, and there are ZERO
#  `$HOME`-relative paths anywhere in them. The single privileged operation left
#  is `ldconfig`, which `build_oracle.sh` ALREADY treats as optional -- it warns
#  "ldconfig failed (root privileges are usually required)" and continues -- and
#  which is unnecessary here because `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` is set below and the
#  loader cache was refreshed at build time.
#
#  WHY THIS MATTERS FOR A FIXTURE IMAGE
#  -----------------------------------
#  The container mounts the repository at /repo. Running as root means the
#  container's root IS host root for any bind mount, so a mistaken write escapes
#  into the checkout with full privilege -- and the frozen-artifact guarantee is
#  the single most important invariant of this migration. The `:ro` flag is the
#  primary control; running unprivileged is the independent second one, and the
#  two fail separately.
#
#  A FIXED, NON-ZERO UID IS USED DELIBERATELY
#  -----------------------------------------
#  The UID/GID are pinned as build arguments rather than left to `useradd`'s next
#  free value, because they appear in the ownership of the named volumes
#  (/build, /data, /out) and must therefore be STABLE across rebuilds -- an image
#  rebuilt with a different UID would no longer own volumes created by the
#  previous one. 1001 is chosen to sit outside the system range and clear of the
#  base image's `ubuntu` account at 1000.
#
#  Override them if your host needs matching ownership on a bind mount:
#      docker compose build --build-arg ACAS_UID=$(id -u) --build-arg ACAS_GID=$(id -g)
#
#  SET-USER-ID HELPERS ARE STRIPPED
#  -------------------------------
#  The base image inherits the distribution's usual set-user-ID binaries (`su`,
#  `mount`, `passwd`, `newgrp`, `chsh`, `chfn`, `gpasswd`, `umount` and the
#  shadow-group helpers). None is needed to compile COBOL or run a scenario, and
#  each is a route by which a process could regain privilege. They are cleared
#  here. This is defence in depth alongside `no-new-privileges:true` on the
#  compose side, which already neuters the set-user-ID bit at the kernel level --
#  both are kept because they fail independently.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  ⭐ m-02.  THIS LAYER VERIFIES THE IDENTITY; IT NO LONGER CREATES A SECOND ONE.
#
#  It used to be a second, ARG-parameterised creator sitting downstream of a
#  hard-coded 1001:1001 creator, reconciling the difference with
#  `groupmod`/`usermod`. That arrangement had three faults the finding named:
#    * TWO SOURCES OF TRUTH for one identity, so the ownership assertions in the
#      earlier layer could describe an identity the image did not finally run as;
#    * the reconciliation could not help when the conflict was with a DIFFERENT
#      NAME at the same id - the base image's own `ubuntu` at 1000:1000 - so the
#      documented `--build-arg ACAS_UID=$(id -u)` invocation aborted the build on
#      any host whose first user is 1000; and
#    * `usermod --uid` on a live account leaves files owned by the OLD uid unless
#      every path is re-chowned, which is a second silent inconsistency.
#  The layer above now owns the whole creation, driven entirely by the ARGs and
#  with the occupied-id case handled explicitly. What remains here is an assertion
#  that the identity is exactly what was asked for, followed by the capability
#  probes - which were always the valuable part of this layer.
RUN set -eu; \
    echo "--- verifying the unprivileged runtime account ${ACAS_USER} (${ACAS_UID}:${ACAS_GID}) ---"; \
    getent group "${ACAS_USER}" > /dev/null \
      || { echo "FATAL: group ${ACAS_USER} does not exist; the identity layer above did not run." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    getent passwd "${ACAS_USER}" > /dev/null \
      || { echo "FATAL: account ${ACAS_USER} does not exist; the identity layer above did not run." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    [ "$(id -u "${ACAS_USER}")" = "${ACAS_UID}" ] \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${ACAS_USER} has uid $(id -u "${ACAS_USER}"), not ACAS_UID=${ACAS_UID}." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    [ "$(id -g "${ACAS_USER}")" = "${ACAS_GID}" ] \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${ACAS_USER} has gid $(id -g "${ACAS_USER}"), not ACAS_GID=${ACAS_GID}." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    [ "$(getent passwd "${ACAS_USER}" | cut -d: -f6)" = "/home/${ACAS_USER}" ] \
      || { echo "FATAL: the home directory of ${ACAS_USER} is $(getent passwd "${ACAS_USER}" | cut -d: -f6), not /home/${ACAS_USER}; ENV HOME below would be wrong." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    echo "    ${ACAS_USER} is ${ACAS_UID}:${ACAS_GID} with home /home/${ACAS_USER}, as the build arguments asked"; \
    \
    echo '--- handing the four writable paths to that account (/repo stays root-owned) ---'; \
    chown -R "${ACAS_UID}:${ACAS_GID}" /build /data /out; \
    chmod 0755 /build /build/bin /data /out; \
    \
    echo '--- clearing set-user-ID / set-group-ID bits ---'; \
    find / -xdev -perm /6000 -type f -print -exec chmod -s {} + ; \
    remaining="$(find / -xdev -perm /6000 -type f | wc -l)"; \
    [ "${remaining}" = '0' ] \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${remaining} set-user-ID/set-group-ID binaries remain." >&2; \
           find / -xdev -perm /6000 -type f >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the account can do everything the harness needs ---'; \
    su -s /bin/bash -c 'test -w /build && test -w /build/bin && test -w /data && test -w /out' "${ACAS_USER}" \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the runtime account cannot write to /build, /build/bin, /data or /out.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    su -s /bin/bash -c 'command -v cobc && command -v presql2 && command -v python3 && command -v mariadb && command -v script' "${ACAS_USER}" \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the runtime account cannot reach cobc, presql2, python3, mariadb or script on its PATH.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    su -s /bin/bash -c 'test -s /usr/local/lib/acas/cobmysqlapi.o' "${ACAS_USER}" \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the runtime account cannot read the published cobmysqlapi.o that step 2 of build_oracle.sh reuses.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    su -s /bin/bash -c 'cd /build && printf "%s\n" "       identification division." "       program-id. p." "       procedure division." "           display \"COBC-AS-USER-OK\"." "           stop run." > /build/.acas-user-probe.cbl && cobc -x -o /build/.acas-user-probe /build/.acas-user-probe.cbl && /build/.acas-user-probe' "${ACAS_USER}" \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the unprivileged account cannot compile and run a COBOL program; the oracle build would fail at run time.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    rm -f /build/.acas-user-probe /build/.acas-user-probe.cbl; \
    \
    echo "OK: ${ACAS_USER} (${ACAS_UID}:${ACAS_GID}) owns /build, /data and /out, can compile COBOL, and set-*-ID helpers are cleared."


#  ENVIRONMENT CONTRACT
#  DATABASE: the client half of the contract the sibling image states. The
#  names must agree across both images and compose. `ACAS_DB_USER` and
#  `ACAS_DB_PASSWORD` are deliberately NOT declared -- an ENV entry is
#  permanent image metadata, which is why BuildKit's `SecretsUsedInArgOrEnv`
#  lint exists; compose supplies both at run time. `ACAS_DB_SOCKET` IS declared
#  empty, meaning TCP, which is a routing choice rather than a secret.
#  COBOL RUNTIME: `COBCPY`/`COB_COPY_DIR` mirror the `../copybooks` that
#  [comp-all.sh:L9-L10] exports; `COB_LIBRARY_PATH` is seeded with the six
#  compile directories in the maintainer's order [comp-all.sh:L15-L32];
#  `COB_EXIT_WAIT=off` removes only a terminal pause, without which every run
#  hangs; `COB_SCREEN_*` are left to [general/general.cbl:L369-L370] itself.

#  ACAS_LEDGERS AND ACAS_BIN ARE BOTH LOAD-BEARING -- AN INTERACTIVE GATE
#  [copybooks/Proc-Get-Env-Set-Files.cob:L20-L28] accepts both from the
#  environment and, if EITHER is blank, displays a message, waits on an ACCEPT
#  and `stop run` -- the same class of hang as the `sys002` trap, reached early
#  because [general/general.cbl:L372] performs `zz020-Get-Program-Args`.
#  `ACAS_LEDGERS` must be ABSOLUTE and carry no trailing delimiter: [:L30-L31]
#  derives the platform delimiter from its first character, and
#  `zz020-Set-the-Paths` prefixes every file name with the pair. `ACAS_BIN` is
#  where the menus look for a backup script [general/general.cbl:L489-L503]; a
#  missing one is NON-FATAL, changing only a displayed field, but setting it
#  makes the behaviour deterministic. TERMINAL: [:L374-L383] `goback`s below 24
#  lines or 80 columns, so TERM, LINES and COLUMNS satisfy that 24x80 floor.
ENV ACAS_DB_HOST=mariadb \
    ACAS_DB_PORT=3306 \
    ACAS_DB_NAME=ACASDB \
    ACAS_DB_SOCKET="" \
    ACAS_REPO=/repo \
    ACAS_BUILD=/build \
    ACAS_DATA=/data \
    ACAS_OUT=/out \
    ACAS_BIN=/build/bin \
    ACAS_LEDGERS=/data \
    ACAS_PRESQL2_PACKAGE=/opt/presql2-package \
    ACAS_COBMYSQLAPI_OBJ=/usr/local/lib/acas/cobmysqlapi.o \
    ACAS_MYSQL_PREFIX=/usr/local/mysql \
    COBCPY=/build/copybooks \
    COB_COPY_DIR=/build/copybooks \
    COB_LIBRARY_PATH=/build/common:/build/general:/build/irs:/build/purchase:/build/sales:/build/stock:/usr/local/lib/gnucobol \
    COB_EXIT_WAIT=off \
    LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/usr/local/lib:/usr/local/mysql/lib \
    TERM=xterm \
    LINES=24 \
    COLUMNS=80


#  WORKING DIRECTORY AND DEFAULT COMMAND
#
#  WORKDIR is /build -- the WRITABLE copy -- and deliberately NEVER /repo.
#  Defaulting to the read-only checkout would invite exactly the writes that
#  would damage the frozen tree, and a `cobc` run there would scatter `.prn`
#  listings and `.so` modules through it.
#
#  BECAUSE /build STARTS EMPTY, NOTHING IS INVOKED RELATIVE TO IT. Every harness
#  script is run by absolute path from the read-only mount --
#  `/repo/harness/<script>` -- for the reason set out in full in the MOUNT POINTS
#  block above. WORKDIR still matters: it is where the compile scripts do their
#  work once the bootstrap has populated the tree, and it keeps any accidental
#  relative write inside the writable volume rather than aimed at the checkout.
#
#  CMD is a plain shell because compose runs the harness scripts explicitly --
#  `build_oracle.sh`, `seed.sh`, `reset_db.sh`, `run_cobol_scenario.sh`,
#  `run_python_scenario.sh`, then `dump_tables.py`, `normalize.py` and
#  `diff_states.py`. No ENTRYPOINT wrapper is used: a wrapper would sit between
#  the scripts and their output, and because
#  [common/comp-common.sh:L59] and [comp-all.sh:L44-L45] both end with an
#  unconditional `exit 0`, `build_oracle.sh` must scan that output verbatim to
#  detect failure. Nothing here may swallow, colourise, filter or reorder it.
#
#  No supervisor and no process manager is installed: a second concurrent
#  scenario against the shared database would break the parity protocol.
#
#  USER is `acas`, and it is the LAST instruction that changes identity, so every
#  layer above installed as root and everything below this line -- the idling
#  service, every `docker compose exec`, every scenario run -- executes
#  unprivileged. HOME is declared explicitly rather than left to be inherited:
#  the base image's config carries `HOME=/root`, which the runtime user cannot
#  write, and several artefacts the frozen programs create are named RELATIVELY
#  -- `acas.param` [common/acas-get-params.cbl:L103] and `fh-logger.txt`
#  [common/fhlogger.cbl:L111] are both plain relative assigns -- so an
#  unwritable HOME is a real failure mode rather than a cosmetic one.
#
#  WORKDIR comes first so that it is created (if it were absent) as root and so
#  the recorded working directory is unambiguous; /build is already acas-owned
#  from the layer above.
#
#  Two consequences worth knowing when driving this image:
#
#    * `docker compose exec gnucobol bash` gives an UNPRIVILEGED shell. For a
#      one-off diagnosis ask for root explicitly with `exec --user root`; the
#      image does not grant it by default and installs no `sudo`.
#    * `apt-get install` inside a running container will fail, and that is
#      intended: the package set is snapshot-pinned and exactly versioned at
#      build time, so an ad-hoc install would make the running arbiter differ
#      from the one that was built.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  ⭐ m-02.  ONE `USER` DIRECTIVE, AND IT IS NUMERIC.
#
#  There used to be TWO - `USER ${ACAS_UID}:${ACAS_GID}` here and a bare
#  `USER acas` three lines further down - and the second silently overrode the
#  first, so the numeric one was dead. They happened to resolve to the same
#  account, which is exactly why the duplication was hard to see and worth
#  removing: nothing warns when a Dockerfile sets USER twice.
#
#  THE NUMERIC FORM IS THE ONE KEPT, deliberately. A name has to be resolved
#  through /etc/passwd inside the container, so it depends on a file the runtime
#  could differ on; a uid:gid pair is what the kernel actually enforces, is what
#  the volume ownership above was set to, and is what `docker compose` reports.
#  The account still exists and is still asserted, so `su`-style diagnosis by name
#  keeps working.
USER ${ACAS_UID}:${ACAS_GID}

WORKDIR /build

#  ⭐ m-02.  `HOME` follows ${ACAS_USER} rather than repeating the literal, and the
#  verification layer above asserts that the account's sixth passwd field really is
#  /home/${ACAS_USER} - so this value cannot drift away from the account it names.
#  It matters because `USER` with a NUMERIC id leaves HOME unset otherwise, and the
#  frozen programs create `acas.param` [common/acas-get-params.cbl:L103] and
#  `fh-logger.txt` [common/fhlogger.cbl:L111] through plain relative assigns.
ENV HOME=/home/${ACAS_USER}

CMD ["bash"]
